 true] [17592186087677 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304994 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186087677 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304995 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186087677 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304996 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186087730 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304782 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186087734 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304786 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186087734 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304787 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186087734 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304788 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186087735 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304784 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186087874 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304933 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186087874 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304941 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186087874 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304942 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186087874 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304943 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186087874 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304944 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186087874 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304945 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186087874 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304946 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186087874 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304947 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186087874 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304948 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186087874 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304949 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186088144 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085462 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186088144 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304216 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186088144 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186088144 1071 "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+2xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+2xy=4$\n- ${y}^{′}+2xy=-y$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186088144 1071 "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n1. $y^{′} + 2xy = 0$\n2. $y^{′} + 2xy = 4$\n3. $y^{′} + 2xy =-y$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186088144 1074 "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each equation in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2xy=4.\n$$\n\nThe presence of the constant term $4$ means the right-hand side is not zero, and so it cannot be rewritten in homogeneous form. Thus, this equation is *not* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2xy=-y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+2xy & =-y \\\\ {y}^{′}+2xy+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+(2x+1)y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186088145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085468 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186088145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304745 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186088145 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186088145 1071 "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=0$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy=y$\n- ${y}^{′}-xy={x}^{3}$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186088145 1071 "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n1. $y^{′} - xy = 0$\n2. $y^{′} - xy = y$\n3. $y^{′} - xy = x^{3}$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186088145 1074 "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=0.\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is already zero, so the equation directly matches the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy=y.\n$$\n\nWe move the term involving $y$ to the left side so that all $y$-terms are together:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-xy & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}-xy-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}-(x+1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis matches the homogeneous form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$  Therefore, this equation *is* homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-xy={x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)={x}^{3}$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186092166 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304826 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186092167 1038 #edb/ref 17592186305037 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186092167 1038 #edb/ref 17592186305038 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186092172 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304842 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186092172 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304967 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186092172 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304970 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186092172 1038 #edb/ref 17592186305008 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186092172 1038 #edb/ref 17592186305009 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186092172 1038 #edb/ref 17592186305010 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186092257 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304828 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186092257 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304834 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186092258 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304831 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186100848 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304971 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186100849 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304774 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186100849 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304775 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186100849 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304776 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186100850 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304778 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186100850 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304804 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186100850 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304805 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186100956 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304813 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186100956 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304972 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186101028 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304798 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186101028 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304803 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186101028 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304806 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186101386 1068 #edb/ref 17592186099452 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186101386 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304692 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186101386 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186101386 1071 "Find the number $c > 0$ such that the average value of $f(x) = 9 + 8x - 3x^{2}$ on the interval $[0, c]$ is equal to $-3$." 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186101386 1071 "Find the number $c>0$ such that the average value of $f(x)=9+8x-3{x}^{2}$ on the interval $[0,c]$ is equal to $-3.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186101386 1074 "To solve for $c,$ we apply the average value formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}\\,{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -3 & =\\frac{1}{c-0}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{c}(9+8x-3{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -3 & =\\frac{1}{c}(9x+4{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}){|}_{0}^{c} \\\\ -3 & =\\frac{1}{c}[(9c+4{c}^{2}-{c}^{3})-(0)] \\\\ -3 & =9+4c-{c}^{2} \\\\ {c}^{2}-4c-12 & =0 \\\\ (c+2)(c-6) & =0 \\\\ c & =-2,\\,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $c>0,$ we conclude that $c=6$ only." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186105831 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304921 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186105831 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304924 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186105832 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304929 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186105836 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304966 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186115191 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304833 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186115191 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304844 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186115191 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304845 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186115225 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304827 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186115315 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304846 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186115315 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304847 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186115315 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304848 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186115396 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304897 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186115398 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304795 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186115398 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304797 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186115398 1038 #edb/ref 17592186305072 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186115778 1068 #edb/ref 17592186113604 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186115778 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304122 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186115778 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186115778 1071 "If $f(t) = \\cos ui + (1 - u^{2})j + 12k$ and $u(t) = t^{3}$ then $(f∘u)^{′}(t) =$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186115778 1071 "If $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,i+(1-{u}^{2})\\,j+12\\,k$ and $u(t)={t}^{3}$ then $(f∘u)^{′}(t)=$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186115778 1074 "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(f∘u)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(u(t)){u}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡u)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(1-{u}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(12)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,i-2u\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,i-2u\\,j \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})=3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘u)^{′}(t) & ={f}^{′}(u(t))\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u(t))\\,i-2(u(t))\\,j)\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{3})\\,i-2({t}^{3})\\,j)\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =-3{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{3}\\,i-6{t}^{5}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186115781 1068 #edb/ref 17592186113622 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186115781 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304263 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186115781 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186115781 1071 "Differentiate $f(t) + g(t)$ with respect to $t$, given that\n\n$f(t)|= ⟨3t - 1, 6 - t, 7t⟩; g(t)|= ⟨t^{2}, 1 - t^{2}, t⟩$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186115781 1071 "Differentiate $f(t)+g(t)$ with respect to $t$, given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨3t-1,\\,6-t,\\,7t⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨{t}^{2},\\,1-{t}^{2},\\,t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186115781 1074 "By the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(f(t)+g(t))^{′}={f}^{′}(t)+{g}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6-t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,-1,\\,7⟩ \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,-2t,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f(t)+g(t))^{′} & ={f}^{′}(t)+{g}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,-1,\\,7⟩+⟨2t,\\,-2t,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t+3,\\,-2t-1,\\,8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186119743 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304835 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186119743 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304908 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186119743 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304910 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186119743 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304911 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186119832 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304802 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186119832 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304809 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186119833 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304810 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186119834 1038 #edb/ref 17592186305044 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186119835 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304814 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186119835 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304824 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186119838 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304829 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186119838 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304830 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186119838 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304843 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186119838 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304935 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186119839 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304934 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186119841 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304918 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186119843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304968 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186119843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304973 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186119843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304977 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186119939 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304909 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186119942 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304932 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186119950 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304800 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186119984 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304807 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186119984 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304808 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186119984 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304811 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186119984 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304812 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120220 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117451 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186120220 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304450 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120220 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120220 1071 "Find a vector that's tangent to the curve $r(t) = ⟨2(t - \\sin t), 2(t - \\cos t)⟩$ at the point where $t = π$." 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186120220 1071 "Find a vector that's tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨2(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,2(t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩$ at the point where $t=\\pi .$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120220 1074 "At the point where $t=\\pi $,  the vector ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nLet's calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[2(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)],\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[2(t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)]⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,2(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ),\\,2(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2(1-(-1)),\\,2(1+0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=\\pi $  is  $⟨4,\\,2⟩$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120226 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117487 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186120226 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304447 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120226 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120226 1071 "Consider the straight line with the canonical equation $\\frac{x}{1} = \\frac{y + 1}{2} = \\frac{z}{3}$. Find the vector equation of the line." 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186120226 1071 "Consider the straight line with the canonical equation $\\frac{x}{1}=\\frac{y+1}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.$ Find the vector equation of the line." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120226 1074 "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{1}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{2}=\\frac{z}{3},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(0,-1,0)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨1,2,3⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117535 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186120234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304506 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120234 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120234 1071 "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t) = ti + t^{2}j$ at an arbitrary point where $t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$." 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186120234 1071 "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j$ at an arbitrary point where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120234 1074 "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}}(\\,i+2t\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117541 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186120235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304551 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120235 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120235 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:\\frac{x-1}{2}=\\frac{y+2}{1}=\\frac{z-1}{3},\\,Π:r\\cdot ⟨1,6,2⟩=19.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120235 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n$l: \\frac{x - 1}{2} = \\frac{y + 2}{1} = \\frac{z - 1}{3}, Π: r \\cdot ⟨1, 6, 2⟩ = 19$." 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186120235 1074 "From the equation of the line $l$, we know that it passes through the point $A(1,-2,1)$ and it is parallel to the vector $v=⟨2,1,3⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨1,-2,1⟩+t⟨2,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+2t,-2+t,1+3t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨1+2t,-2+t,1+3t⟩$ into the equation of the plane, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨1,6,2⟩ & =19 \\\\ ⟨1+2t,-2+t,1+3t⟩\\cdot ⟨1,6,2⟩ & =19 \\\\ (1+2t)+6(-2+t)+2(1+3t) & =19 \\\\ 1+2t-12+6t+2+6t & =19 \\\\ 14t & =28 \\\\ t & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =⟨1+2(2),-2+2,1+3(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,0,7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get $P(5,0,7).$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117547 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186120236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304525 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120236 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120236 1071 "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t) = e^{-t}p(t)$ at the point where $t = 0$, if $p(0) = i + j + k$ and $p^{′}(0) = i - j$." 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186120236 1071 "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)={e}^{-t}p(t)$ at the point where $t=0$, if $p(0)=\\,i+\\,j+k$ and ${p}^{′}(0)=\\,i-\\,j.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120236 1074 "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})p(t)+{e}^{-t}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & =-{e}^{-t}p(t)+{e}^{-t}{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =-{e}^{0}p(0)+{e}^{0}{p}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =-(\\,i+\\,j+k)+(\\,i-\\,j) \\\\  & =-2j-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(0)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(0) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(0)}{‖{r}^{′}(0)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(-2j-k) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}j-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=0$ is $T(0)=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}j-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}k.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117581 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186120242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304582 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120242 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120242 1071 "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $B(-2, 23, 14)$ and is parallel to the lines $r = ⟨ - 3, 5, 1⟩ + s⟨ - 3, 5, 4⟩$ and $r = ⟨ - 30, 55, 25⟩ + t⟨4, 7,-1⟩$." 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186120242 1071 "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $B(-2,23,14)$ and is parallel to the lines $r=⟨-3,5,1⟩+s⟨-3,5,4⟩$ and $r=⟨-30,55,25⟩+t⟨4,7,-1⟩.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120242 1074 "The direction vectors of the lines are ${v}_{1}=⟨-3,5,4⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨4,7,-1⟩$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $B(-2,23,14)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $b=⟨-2,23,14⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =b+s{v}_{1}+t{v}_{2} \\\\ r & =⟨-2,23,14⟩+s⟨-3,5,4⟩+t⟨4,7,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117587 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186120243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304107 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120243 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120243 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(8, 3,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the line\n$\\frac{x + 2}{4} = \\frac{y - 1}{3} = \\frac{z - 2}{-1}$." 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186120243 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(8,3,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\frac{x+2}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z-2}{-1}.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120243 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨8,3,-1⟩.$ From the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨4,3,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨4,3,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨4,3,-1⟩ & =⟨8,3,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨4,3,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨4,3,-1⟩ & =42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨4,3,-1⟩ & =42 \\\\ 4x+3y-z & =42 \\\\ 4x+3y-z-42 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120249 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117623 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186120249 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304560 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120249 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186120249 1074 "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-2,3,1)$ and $Q(5,-1,4).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨5,-1,4⟩-⟨-2,3,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨7,-4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-2)}{7}=\\frac{y-3}{-4}=\\frac{z-1}{3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{7}=\\frac{y-3}{-4}=\\frac{z-1}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186147511 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304919 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186147511 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304920 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186147511 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304922 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186147511 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304923 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186147513 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304925 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186147513 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304926 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186147513 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304927 1319