592186045585 true] [17592186167204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164215 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186167204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304265 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167204 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167204 1071 "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using the following numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =({x}^{2}+y)\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={x}^{3},$ find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167204 1071 "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using the following numerical method:\n$Δy|= (x^{2} + y) \\cdot Δx; y_{new}|= y + Δy$\nIf the exact solution is $y_{exact}(x) = x^{3}$, find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to $x_{new} = x + Δx$." 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186167204 1074 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution of the initial value problem is $y(x)={x}^{3}.$ Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =({x}^{2}+{y}_{\\text{exact}}(x))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+{x}^{3})\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & =(x+Δx)^{3} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot Δx+3x\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for step $n$ starting at $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot Δx+3x\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{3}]-[{x}^{3}+({x}^{2}+{x}^{3})\\cdot Δx] \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-{x}^{3}+(3{x}^{2}-({x}^{2}+{x}^{3}))\\cdot Δx+3x\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{3} \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})Δx+3x(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164227 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186167206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164230 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186167206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164233 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186167206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304142 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304519 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304760 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167206 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167206 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167206 1071 "${x^{′}(t) =-2x(t); y^{′}(t) = 3y(t)$\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186167206 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/09/0948dac4f8a192018baee1339d7334d573592d7b7bf4383c1297cc2431e51201.png)" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167209 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164239 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186167209 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164240 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186167209 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164241 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186167209 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304228 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167209 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304382 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167209 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304459 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167209 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167209 1071 "A numerical method is used to approximate a solution to an initial value problem, resulting in the table below. If the exact solution is $y(x) = e^{-x}$, find, rounded to three decimal places, the global truncation error at each step.\n\n| $n$ | $x_{n}$ | $y_{n}$ | $e_{n}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $0.762$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $0.558$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.6$ | $0.353$ | {{field-3}} |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186167209 1071 "A numerical method is used to approximate a solution to an initial value problem, resulting in the table below. If the exact solution is $y(x)={e}^{-x},$ find, rounded to three decimal places, the global truncation error at each step.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $0.762$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $0.558$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.6$ | $0.353$ | {{field-3}} |" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167209 1074 "The global truncation error (GTE) is the total error accumulated after several steps of a numerical method. The GTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from ${x}_{n-1}$ to ${x}_{n}={x}_{n-1}+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n{e}_{n}=y({x}_{n})-{y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $y({x}_{n})$ is the exact value at ${x}_{n},$ and ${y}_{n}$ is the numerical value produced after $n$ steps.\n\nWe are given the numerical values ${y}_{n}$ produced at each step in the table. Now, we compute the exact value $y({x}_{n})$ at each value of ${x}_{n}:$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{0}=0$ is $y(0)={e}^{-0}=1.$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{1}=0.2$ is $y(0.2)={e}^{-0.2}.$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{2}=0.4$ is $y(0.4)={e}^{-0.4}.$\n\n- The exact value at ${x}_{2}=0.6$ is $y(0.6)={e}^{-0.6}.$\n\nLet's add these values to the table.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | $y({x}_{n})$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $0.762$ | ${e}^{-0.2}$ |  |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $0.558$ | ${e}^{-0.4}$ |  |\n| $3$ | $0.6$ | $0.353$ | ${e}^{-0.6}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we find the GTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- The GTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{1} & =y({x}_{1})-{y}_{1} \\\\  & ={e}^{-0.2}-0.762 \\\\  & ≈0.057.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & =y({x}_{2})-{y}_{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-0.4}-0.558 \\\\  & ≈0.112.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{3} & =y({x}_{3})-{y}_{3} \\\\  & ={e}^{-0.6}-0.353 \\\\  & ≈0.196.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | $y({x}_{n})$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $0.762$ | ${e}^{-0.2}$ | $0.057$ |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $0.558$ | ${e}^{-0.4}$ | $0.112$ |\n| $3$ | $0.6$ | $0.353$ | ${e}^{-0.6}$ | $0.196$ |" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164259 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186167213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304414 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167213 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167213 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167213 1071 "${x^{′}(t) = 2x(t); y^{′}(t) = 3y(t)$\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186167213 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/f5/f54544342b11a60ec784160f582b5bdd03a28abee01767a732bef6ac03d9ec62.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{3t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{3t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{2t})^{′}}=\\frac{3{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}}{2{c}_{1}{e}^{2t}}=\\frac{3{c}_{2}}{2{c}_{1}}{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to \\pm \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/eb/eba955015a4b919c65dc24facb0baa6e745df134feac32feeae07c99d40ff4ec.png)" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164265 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186167214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164266 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186167214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164267 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186167214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304132 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304353 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304738 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167214 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167214 1071 "An initial value problem has the exact solution $y_{exact}(x) = \\sin x$. A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to the initial value problem, resulting in the table below, where $y$ denotes the approximate solution at each step, and $τ_{n}$ is the local truncation error at each step. At each step, the exact value is used to approximate the new value.\n\nCalculate the local truncation error at each step. Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | $τ_{n}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.25$ | $0.250$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.5$ | $0.483$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.75$ | $0.688$ | {{field-3}} |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186167214 1071 "An initial value problem has the exact solution ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to the initial value problem, resulting in the table below, where $y$ denotes the approximate solution at each step, and ${τ}_{n}$ is the local truncation error at each step. At each step, the exact value is used to approximate the new value. \n\nCalculate the local truncation error at each step. Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${τ}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.25$ | $0.250$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.5$ | $0.483$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.75$ | $0.688$ | {{field-3}} |" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167214 1074 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the LTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- After the first step, we have ${y}_{\\text{new}}=0.250.$ So, the LTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{1} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.25)-0.250 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.25)-0.250 \\\\  & ≈-0.003.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the second step, we have ${y}_{\\text{new}}=0.483.$ So, the LTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{2} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.5)-0.483 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.5)-0.483 \\\\  & ≈-0.004.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the third step, we have ${y}_{\\text{new}}=0.688.$ So, the LTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{3} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.75)-0.688 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.75)-0.688 \\\\  & ≈-0.006.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${τ}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.25$ | $0.250$ | $-0.003$ |\n| $2$ | $0.5$ | $0.483$ | $-0.004$ |\n| $3$ | $0.75$ | $0.688$ | $-0.006$ |" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164339 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186167221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304368 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167221 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167221 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167221 1071 "${x^{′}(t) =-x(t); y^{′}(t) = 4y(t)$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186167221 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\