n{\\begin{aligned}x=4(2t-1){e}^{3t} \\\\ y=8t{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-1.79$ | $-4$ | $-4.37$ |\n| $y$ | $-0.89$ | $0$ | $2.92$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6c/6cc714f7f703eaebf31983882cbe988742ea47a9e280c7fbee1f44c877952b63.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/26/26ab713a8961e5668ee3663e0e5ac40ffbd6ea1dd02416ede0e2cbaaad4b63bf.png)" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164371 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186167225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304547 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167225 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167225 1071 "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nA complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as \n$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$\nwhere ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167225 1071 "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n$x^{′}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t) + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nA complementary solution to this system is given by\n$x_{c}(t) = c_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos (2t) \\\\ \\sin (2t)\\end{bmatrix} + c_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin (2t) \\\\ \\cos (2t)\\end{bmatrix}, c_{1}, c_{2} ∈ R$.\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as\n$x(t) = x_{c}(t) + x_{p}(t)$,\nwhere $x_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution $x_{p}(t)$." 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186167225 1074 "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is constant. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also constant, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164429 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186167232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164430 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186167232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304189 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167232 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304730 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167232 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167232 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y = y(x), z = z(x)$:\n${y^{′} = x + 3y - z^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}y(-2) = 1 \\\\ z^{′} = x - y + 2z,\\end{vmatrix}z(-2) = 1$\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(0)$ and $z(0)$.$y(0) \\approx$\n{{field-1}},\n$z(0) \\approx$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186167232 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=x+3y-{z}^{2},\\, & y(-2)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=x-y+2z,\\, & z(-2)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(0)$ and $z(0).$\n\n$y(0)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(0)≈$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186167232 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=-2$ and asked to find their values at $x=0.$ The distance from $x=-2$ to $x=0$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{0-(-2)}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(-2)=1$ and $z(-2)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+3y-{z}^{2}=-2+3\\cdot 1-{1}^{2}=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x-y+2z=-2-1+2\\cdot 1=-1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-1\\cdot 1=-1 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-2+1=-1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+(-1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $-1$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+3y-{z}^{2}=-1+3\\cdot 1-{0}^{2}=2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x-y+2z=-1-1+2\\cdot 0=-2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-2\\cdot 1=-2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+1=0 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+2=3 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+(-2)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-2$ |\n| $0$ | $3$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(0)≈3$\nand\n$z(0)≈-2.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186176088 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304849 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186176088 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304850 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186176088 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304856 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186198473 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304836 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186198473 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304837 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186198473 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304838 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186198474 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304839 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186198474 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304840 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186198474 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304841 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186198475 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304868 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186198475 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304870 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186198475 1038 #edb/ref 17592186304871 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186217783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216543 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186217783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304372 13194139533414 