ion to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{-2x}$, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.$y(x) =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229630 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=4x+2.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229630 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +2(\\alpha x+\\beta )=4x+2\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha x+(\\alpha +2\\beta )=4x+2\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +2\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+2x.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228044 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229631 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304378 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229631 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229631 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=8x-4,\\,y(0)=\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=8x-4\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229631 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} - 4y = 8x - 4, y(0) = \\frac{3}{2}$\n\nGiven that the differential equation\n$y^{′} - 4y = 8x - 4$\nhas the complementary solution $y_{c} = Ae^{4x}$, solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x) =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229631 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha -4(\\alpha x+\\beta )=8x-4\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-4\\alpha x+(\\alpha -4\\beta )=8x-4\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -4\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\,\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}-2x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=\\frac{3}{2}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{4(0)}-2(0)+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ A+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{4x}-2x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229633 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228046 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229633 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304610 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229633 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229633 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n$y^{′} + 3y = 6e^{-3x}$,\nhas the complementary solution $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{-3x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.$y_{p}(x) =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229633 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=6{e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229633 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{-3x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-3x}-3x{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x})+3\\alpha x{e}^{-3x} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ (1-3x+3x)\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=6x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229636 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228049 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229636 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228050 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229636 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304200 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229636 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304536 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229636 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229636 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$3\\frac{dy}{dx} - 6y = 4\\cos (2x)$.\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{2x}$, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.$y(x) = Ae^{2x} + ($\n{{field-1}}\n$) \\cdot \\cos (2x) + ($\n{{field-2}}\n$) \\cdot \\sin (2x)$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229636 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=A{e}^{2x}\\,+\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229636 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n3(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-6(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(-6\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-6\\alpha -6\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha +6\\beta =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -6\\alpha -6\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{3},\\beta =\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+(-\\frac{1}{3})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(\\frac{1}{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229638 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228052 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229638 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304460 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229638 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229638 1071 "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method **unstable**, given that $\\lambda =20$ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\n- $Δx=0.05$\n- $Δx=0.12$\n- $Δx=0.2$\n- $Δx=0.25$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229638 1071 "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method **unstable**, given that $λ = 20$ is the parameter of the test equation $y^{′} = - λy$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\ge 0, λ > 0$, and step size $Δx$?\n\n1. $Δx = 0.05$\n2. $Δx = 0.12$\n3. $Δx = 0.2$\n4. $Δx = 0.25$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(λΔx) = 1 - λΔx$." 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229638 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nIn our case, where $\\lambda =20,$ this region simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & <20Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{2}{20} \\\\ 0 & <Δx<0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, of the given step sizes:\n\n- $Δx=0.05$ *satisfies* the inequality,\n\n- $Δx=0.12$ *doesn't satisfy* the inequality,\n\n- $Δx=0.2$ *doesn't satisfy* the inequality, and\n\n- $Δx=0.25$ *doesn't satisfy* the inequality.\n\nHence, Euler's method is unstable for the step sizes $Δx=0.12,$ $Δx=0.2,$ and $Δx=0.25.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II, III, and IV only\"." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229639 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228058 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229639 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304246 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229639 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229639 1071 "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation $y^{′} = - λy$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\ge 0, λ = 5$, and step size $Δx$?\n\n1. The update formula can be written as $y_{n + 1} = (1 - 5Δx)^{n}y_{n}$.\n2. The method is stable for $\\mid 1 - 5Δx \\mid < 1$.\n3. The method is stable for step size $Δx = 0.2$.\n4. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx = 0.25$." 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229639 1071 "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =5,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\n- The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}=(1-5Δx)^{n}{y}_{n}.$\n- The method is stable for $|1-5Δx|<1.$\n- The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$\n- The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.25.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229639 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-5{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-5{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-5Δx){y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-5Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-5Δx)((1-5Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & =(1-5Δx)^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & =(1-5Δx)^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =(1-5Δx)^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-5Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-5Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-5Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-5Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <5Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.25$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229640 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228064 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229640 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304153 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229640 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229640 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$y^{′} - y = 2e^{2x}$.\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{x}$, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.$y(x) =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229640 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229640 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $2{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{2x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha {e}^{2x}-\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+2{e}^{2x}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229642 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228066 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229642 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304565 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229642 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229642 1071 "For which of the following values is Euler's method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.67$\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.20$\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.80$\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.25$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229642 1071 "For which of the following values is Euler's method stable, given that $λ$ is the parameter of the test equation $y^{′} = - λy$ with $y = y(x), y(0) = 1, x \\ge 0, λ > 0$, and step size $Δx$?\n\n1. $λΔx = 0.67$\n2. $λΔx = 1.20$\n3. $λΔx = 1.80$\n4. $λΔx = 2.25$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(λΔx) = 1 - λΔx$." 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229642 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$ Of the given values:\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.67$ falls *within* the given region,\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.20$ falls *within* the given region,\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.80$ falls *within* the given region, and\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.25$ falls *outside* the given region.\n\nHence, Euler's method is stable for the values $\\lambda Δx=0.67,$ $\\lambda Δx=1.20,$ and $\\lambda Δx=1.80.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\"." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229644 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228078 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229644 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304531 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229644 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229644 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + 3y = 6$.\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{-3x}$, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.$y(x) =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229644 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=6.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229644 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+3\\beta =6\n$$\n\nSolving this equation gives $\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+2.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229646 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228085 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229646 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304332 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229646 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229646 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2y = 7\\cos x$\nhas the complementary solution $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{-2x}$, find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ to this equation.$y_{p}(x) =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229646 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229646 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-\\alpha +2\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha +\\beta =7\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{14}{5},\\beta =\\frac{7}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{14}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+\\frac{7}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229648 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228087 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229648 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304120 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229648 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229648 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+7y=3x+4.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229648 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$y^{′} + 7y = 3x + 4$.\n\nFind the complementary solution $y_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229648 1074 "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+7{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-7{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-7\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -7\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int -7\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =-7x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{-7x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{-7x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{-7x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-7x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229651 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228100 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229651 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304252 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229651 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229651 1071 "For which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.25$\n- $\\lambda Δx=1$\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.5$\n- $\\lambda Δx=9$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δ)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229651 1071 "For which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $λ$ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n1. $λΔx = 0.25$\n2. $λΔx = 1$\n3. $λΔx = 1.5$\n4. $λΔx = 9$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n$R(λΔ) = \\frac{1}{1 + λΔx}$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229651 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the implicit Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the Implicit Euler Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.25,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.25)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.25}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.25}| \\\\  & =0.8<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+1}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{2}| \\\\  & =0.5<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.5)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+1.5}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{2.5}| \\\\  & =0.4<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(9)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+9}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{10}| \\\\  & =0.1<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV\".\n\nNote that, since the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229654 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228113 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229654 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228114 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229654 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304098 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229654 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304557 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229654 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229654 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$3\\frac{dy}{dx} - 9y = 6\\sin (2x)$.\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{3x}$, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.$y(x) = Ae^{3x} + ($\n{{field-1}}\n$) \\cdot \\cos (2x) + ($\n{{field-2}}\n$) \\cdot \\sin (2x)$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 false] [17592186229654 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-9y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=A{e}^{3x}\\,+\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186229654 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n3\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-9{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n3(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-9(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(6\\beta -9\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-6\\alpha -9\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6\\beta -9\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -6\\alpha -9\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{4}{13},\\beta =-\\frac{6}{13}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{4}{13}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\frac{6}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+(-\\frac{4}{13})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-\\frac{6}{13})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186302453 1081 "\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}+\\frac{28}{3}{e}^{x}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186302453 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186302453 1084 #uuid "003894d7-c1f0-5659-bf28-e65dae8b206d" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186302454 1081 "6x-2y+z-10=0" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186302454 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186302454 1084 #uuid "0069db2e-d01f-5ba7-85d5-c68688366845" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186302455 1081 "{\\begin{aligned}x=2+2t \\\\ y=1+4t \\\\ z=-1+3t\\end{aligned}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186302455 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186302455 1084 #uuid "006d023d-d46a-561d-b3fd-bba07a9fea2b" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186302456 1081 "3.9" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186302456 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186302456 1084 #uuid "008b3934-16c0-5713-8003-906ae4152fd3" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186302457 1081 "105" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186302457 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186302457 1084 #uuid "009434b5-16c2-5f01-88f4-941f73cce566" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186302458 1081 "\\frac{10(s+3)}{((s+3)^{2}+25)^{2}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186302458 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186302458 1084 #uuid "00b237ee-1954-5652-aad1-50ef0adf539e" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186302459 1081 "images/96/966fab8b4260c34d381bed9b36a28e38711de8c77dc294c7a2c6f29d5cd76f36.png" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186302459 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186302459 1084 #uuid "00d3df36-55d