f 17592186304623 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304772 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304687 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304772 1069 "q-328407" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304772 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304772 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{4{y}^{3}+11y-5}{{y}^{2}+2}\\le 4y.$\n\n$y$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304772 1072 #uuid "c087976e-98c6-5188-99a8-b684a47b94f5" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304772 1074 "We know that the denominator ${y}^{2}+2$ is positive for all values of $y.$ So, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by ${y}^{2}+2$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{y}^{3}+11y-5}{{y}^{2}+2} & \\le 4y \\\\ ({y}^{2}+2)\\cdot \\frac{4{y}^{3}+11y-5}{{y}^{2}+2} & \\le ({y}^{2}+2)\\cdot 4y \\\\ 4{y}^{3}+11y-5 & \\le 4{y}^{3}+8y \\\\ 11y-5 & \\le 8y \\\\ 3y-5 & \\le 0 \\\\ y & \\le \\,\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304773 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304492 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304773 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304550 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304773 1069 "q-328412" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304773 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304773 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{9-2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3}>\\frac{x-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3}.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304773 1072 #uuid "44f8e865-f451-52e4-81bc-a8ded20c253d" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304773 1074 "Note that the denominator $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3$ is always positive. We know this because\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le 1\\,⇒\\,2\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\le 4.\n$$\n\nSo, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9-2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3} & >\\frac{x-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3} \\\\ (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3)\\cdot \\frac{9-2x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3} & >(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3)\\cdot \\frac{x-1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3} \\\\ 9-2x & >x-1 \\\\ -3x & >-10 \\\\ x & <\\,\\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304774 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304166 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304774 1069 "q-328419" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304774 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304774 1071 "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5x+{75}^{∘})=-1\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx={a}^{∘}+n\\cdot {36}^{∘},\n$$\n\n \nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value $a$ in the range $[{0}^{∘},{36}^{∘}).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304774 1072 #uuid "de780784-b180-51d0-bed1-a7bb3f540b0b" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304774 1074 "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+{75}^{∘} & ={135}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the above equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & ={135}^{∘}-{75}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ 5x & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={12}^{∘}+n\\cdot {36}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a=12.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304775 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304699 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304775 1069 "q-328425" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304775 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304775 1071 "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(4x+{30}^{∘})=0\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a+n\\cdot {45}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $a$ in the range $[{0}^{∘},{45}^{∘}).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304775 1072 #uuid "a1c4ab1d-04a9-5d5e-b46a-0f4da37c94a7" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304775 1074 "The given equation is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x+{30}^{∘})=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+{30}^{∘} & ={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the above equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ={90}^{∘}-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ 4x & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={15}^{∘}+n\\cdot {45}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a={15}^{∘}.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304776 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304445 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304776 1069 "q-328434" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304776 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304776 1071 "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-1\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a\\pi +2n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $a$ in the range $[0,2).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304776 1072 #uuid "248aefe9-c6c9-52fa-8897-a63b0cb37188" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304776 1074 "The given equation is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=-1.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the above equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a=\\frac{1}{6}.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304777 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304319 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304777 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304723 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304777 1069 "q-328439" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304777 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304777 1071 "The center of the hyperbola $16{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}-54y=-63$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304777 1072 #uuid "9a7281ca-5d52-5291-98ac-5167d073adb4" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304777 1074 "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nTo rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}-54y & =-63 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}-[\\,9{y}^{2}+54y\\,] & =-63 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}-[\\,9({y}^{2}+6y)\\,] & =-63 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}-[\\,9((y+3)^{2}-9)\\,] & =-63 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}-9(y+3)^{2}+81 & =-63 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}-9(y+3)^{2} & =-63-81 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}-9(y+3)^{2} & =-144 \\\\ \\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{{4}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-(-3))^{2}}{{4}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center of the hyperbola is $(\\,0,-3\\,).$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304778 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304505 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304778 1069 "q-328452" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304778 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304778 1071 "Consider the following trigonometric equation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x)=-2\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation is\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2\\pi }{9}+\\frac{2\\pi n}{3},\\,x=a\\pi +\\frac{2\\pi n}{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $a$ in the range $[0,\\frac{2}{3}).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304778 1072 #uuid "9cefe942-33b9-551a-b9fd-33c835ab67ab" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304778 1074 "First, note that the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n3x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n,\\,3x=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n, & \\,3x & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\pi }{9}+\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}, & \\,x & =\\frac{4\\pi }{9}+\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $a=\\frac{4}{9}.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304779 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304568 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304779 1069 "q-328454" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304779 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304779 1071 "The table below gives the values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p,$ where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. Find the value of $z$ such that $P(Z\\le z)=0.90.$ Use the table below and do not round your answer.\n\n| $\\,p\\,$ | $0.65$ | $0.70$ | $0.75$ | $0.80$ | $0.85$ | $0.90$ | $0.95$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.3853\\,$ | $\\,0.5244\\,$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ |\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304779 1072 #uuid "cd6cb4c5-e422-58ee-a432-48a9084bafbc" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304779 1074 "First, we express the given information in terms of $Φ:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\le z) & =0.90 \\\\ Φ(z) & =0.90\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we are looking for the value of $z$ such that\n\n$$\nz={Φ}^{-1}(0.90).\n$$\n\nWe look for the column in the table that corresponds to $p=0.90,$ and we find that\n\n$$\np=0.90\\,⇒\\,{Φ