ation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+12x+4y & =28 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(0)^{2}+12x+4(0) & =28 \\\\ {x}^{2}+12x & =28 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+12x+36)-36 & =28 \\\\ (x+6)^{2}-36 & =28 \\\\ (x+6)^{2} & =64 \\\\ x+6 & =\\pm 8 \\\\ x & =-6\\pm 8 \\\\ x & =-14,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA(-14,0)\\,\\text{and}\\,B(2,0).\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304355 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304783 1069 "q-328505" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304783 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304783 1071 "The polynomial \n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12\n$$\n\n \ncan be expressed as\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x-3)\\cdot P(x).\n$$\n\nFind the polynomial $P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304783 1072 #uuid "5c8e3cf5-7e90-5470-a693-ef40ffd528ca" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304783 1074 "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12.$ We know that $(x-2)$ and $(x-3)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-2)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $16$ | $-12$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $2$ | $-4$ | $-10$ | $12$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12}{x-2}={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12=(x-2)({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-3)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $3$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-5x+6}{x-3}={x}^{2}+x-2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+16x-12=(x-2)(x-3)({x}^{2}+x-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(x)={x}^{2}+x-2=(x+2)(x-1).\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304784 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304277 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304784 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304408 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304784 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304452 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304784 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304472 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304784 1069 "q-328517" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304784 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304784 1071 "From **top to bottom**, the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-8y+11=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-3}}$,$\n{{field-4}}$)\\,$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304784 1072 #uuid "a07df4a2-bd04-5c67-b80a-8ad3bb2e7bcd" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304784 1074 "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-8y+11 & =0 \\\\ 4(0)^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\cdot 0-8y+11 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-8y+11 & =0 \\\\ [{y}^{2}-8y]+11 & =0 \\\\ [(y-4)^{2}-16]+11 & =0 \\\\ (y-4)^{2}-5 & =0 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =5 \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{5} \\\\ y & =4\\pm \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA\\,(0,4+\\sqrt{5})\\,\\text{and}\\,B\\,(0,4-\\sqrt{5}).\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304785 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304188 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304785 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304325 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304785 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304335 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304785 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304701 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304785 1069 "q-328522" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304785 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304785 1071 "Let $p(x)={x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}-9x+18,$ and $p(3)=p(-3)=0.$ In **ascending order**, the distinct real roots of $p(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}, \n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}, \n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-3}}, \n\n$\\,x=$\n{{field-4}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304785 1072 #uuid "72d3f00d-c15b-5159-9474-aa1f54838d55" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304785 1074 "Since $p(3)=p(-3)=0,$ by factor theorem $(x-3)$ and $(x+3)$ are both factors of $p(x).$\nTo factor $p(x)$ completely we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x-3)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-3)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-11$ | $-9$ | $18$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $12$ | $3$ | $-18$ |\n|  | $1$ | $4$ | $1$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}-9x+18}{x-3}={x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+x-6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}-9x+18=(x-3)({x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+x-6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+3)$ is also a factor of $p(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $({x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+x-6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division once more.\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $1$ | $4$ | $1$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-3$ | $-3$ | $6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+x-6}{x+3}={x}^{2}+x-2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}-9x+18=(x-3)(x+3)({x}^{2}+x-2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}-9x+18=(x-3)(x+3)(x-1)(x+2).\n$$\n\nWe can now equate each factor to zero and solve for $x$ to find the four roots. In ascending order, the roots are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-3\\,,\\,x=-2\\,,\\,x=1\\,,\\,x=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304093 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304097 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304484 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304786 1069 "q-328525" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304786 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304786 1071 "From **top to bottom**, the ellipse $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,-\\frac{1}{2})\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304786 1072 #uuid "5fa953ef-5f3c-5422-a364-50b75a5dd976" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304786 1074 "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-3)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{9}{12}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 9\\,(\\frac{3}{4})+(y+2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ \\frac{27}{4}+(y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{36}{4} \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =-2\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA(0,\\,-\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{and}\\,B(0,\\,-\\frac{7}{2}).\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304787 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304321 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304787 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304462 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304787 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304584 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304787 1069 "q-328527" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304787 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304787 1071 "From **top to bottom**, the ellipse $\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\frac{2}{5})\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304787 1072 #uuid "cac8409f-9821-5d79-81e5-35b84cdccd69" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304787 1074 "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{16}{25}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ 256+25(y+2)^{2} & =400 \\\\ 25(y+2)^{2} & =144 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{144}{25} \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm \\frac{12}{5} \\\\ y & =-2\\pm \\frac{12}{5} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{22}{5},\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA(0,\\frac{2}{5})\\,\\text{and}\\,B(0,-\\frac{22}{5}).\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304788 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304311 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304788 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304376 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304788 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304714 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304788 1069 "q-328528" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304788 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304788 1071 "From **top to bottom**, the ellipse $\\frac{(x-8)^{2}}{100}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{9}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis at the points $A$ and $B.$ Find the coordinates of these points.\n\n$A($\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\frac{4}{5})\\,$\n\nand \n\n$\\,B($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304788 1072 #uuid "42289c0d-5c1d-5194-930c-c838b65a8c91" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304788 1074 "To calculate the $y$-intercept, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $y.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-8)^{2}}{100}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(0-8)^{2}}{100}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{64}{100}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ 144+25(y+1)^{2} & =225 \\\\ 25(y+1)^{2} & =81 \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =\\frac{81}{25} \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ y & =-1\\pm \\frac{9}{5} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{14}{5},\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are\n\n$$\nA(0,\\frac{4}{5})\\,\\text{and}\\,B(0,-\\frac{14}{5}).\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304789 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304131 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304789 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304763 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304789 1069 "q-328530" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304789 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304789 1071 "Solve the inequality\n$\\frac{3x+4}{ln\\,({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}}>\\frac{x+10}{ln\\,({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}}.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304789 1072 #uuid "f3e413c3-35de-5999-a08f-53da2546a391" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304789 1074 "We know that the denominator $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}$ is positive for all values of $x.$ So, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x+4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}} & >\\frac{x+10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}} \\\\ (\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x})\\cdot \\frac{3x+4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}} & >(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x})\\cdot \\frac{x+10}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+{e}^{x}} \\\\ 3x+4 & >x+10 \\\\ 2x & >6 \\\\ x & >\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304790 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304604 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304790 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304630 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304790 1069 "q-328531" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304790 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304790 1071 "Solve the inequality\n$\\frac{2x+7}{|x|+{x}^{2}+1}\\ge \\frac{x+4}{|x|+{x}^{2}+1}.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304790 1072 #uuid "a25e1e0a-95ff-5862-b01f-18afd3116acd" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304790 1074 "We know that the denominator $|x|+{x}^{2}+1$ is positive for all values of $x.$ So, we can multiply both sides of the inequality by $|x|+{x}^{2}+1$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+7}{|x|+{x}^{2}+1} & \\ge \\frac{x+4}{|x|+{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ (|x|+{x}^{2}+1)\\cdot \\frac{2x+7}{|x|+{x}^{2}+1} & \\ge (|x|+{x}^{2}+1)\\cdot \\frac{x+4}{|x|+{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ 2x+7 & \\ge x+4 \\\\ x & \\ge -3 \\\\ x & \\ge \\,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304791 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304309 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304791 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304562 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304791 1069 "q-328549" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304791 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304791 1071 "Solve the inequality\n$(2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9})(-7x-5)<0.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304791 1072 #uuid "0fd88dd4-0650-544b-8520-d8dc46289aca" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304791 1074 "Note that the factor $2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}$ is always positive for all real numbers $x.$\n\nSince $2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9})(-7x-5)}{\\,2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}\\,} & <\\frac{0}{\\,2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}\\,} \\\\ -7x-5 & <0 \\\\ -7x & <5 \\\\ x & >\\,-\\frac{5}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304792 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304209 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304792 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304347 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304792 1069 "q-328551" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304792 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304792 1071 "Solve the inequality\n$(|x-2|+3)(4x-1)\\le 0.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304792 1072 #uuid "882322f3-f8f5-5b93-b51c-e1e16bf594c8" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304792 1074 "Note that the factor $|x-2|+3$ is always positive for all real numbers $x.$\n\nSince $|x-2|+3$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(|x-2|+3)(4x-1)}{|x-2|+3} & \\le \\frac{0}{\\,|x-2|+3\\,} \\\\ 4x-1 & \\le 0 \\\\ 4x & \\le 1 \\\\ x & \\le \\,\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304793 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304084 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304793 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304268 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304793 1069 "q-328553" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304793 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304793 1071 "Solve the inequality\n$({e}^{2x}+{x}^{4})(5x-1)<0.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304793 1072 #uuid "8c68358c-068c-59e2-991f-a3d824b9c4fb" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304793 1074 "Since ${e}^{2x}+{x}^{4}$ is always positive, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{({e}^{2x}+{x}^{4})(5x-1)}{{e}^{2x}+{x}^{4}} & <\\frac{0}{{e}^{2x}+{x}^{4}} \\\\ 5x-1 & <0 \\\\ 5x & <1 \\\\ x & <\\,\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304794 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304473 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304794 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304662 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304794 1069 "q-328558" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304794 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304794 1071 "Solve the inequality\n$(-4{x}^{2}-1)(3x-2)<0.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304794 1072 #uuid "a6beae78-3fdd-529b-a19f-103a8ec233f1" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304794 1074 "Since $-4{x}^{2}-1$ is always negative, we can divide both sides of the inequality by this quantity, but we have to remember to flip the sign of the inequality. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4{x}^{2}-1)(3x-2) & <0 \\\\ \\frac{(-4{x}^{2}-1)(3x-2)}{-4{x}^{2}-1} & >\\frac{0}{-4{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ 3x-2 & >0 \\\\ x & >\\,\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304795 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304144 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304795 1069 "q-32860" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304795 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304795 1071 "If $g(u)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡u \\\\ 5 \\\\ {u}^{2}\\end{aligned}]$ and $u(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$, then  $(g∘u)^{′}(t)=$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304795 1072 #uuid "8d3c1bb9-bc23-5441-85c4-926900a42dbb" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304795 1074 "By the chain rule, we have \n\n$$\n(f∘u)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(u(t)){u}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(u) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡u \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\,\\,5\\,\\, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\,\\,{u}^{2}\\,\\,\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{u} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2u\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)=\\frac{1}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g∘u)^{′}(t) & =g′(u(t))u′(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{u(t)} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2u(t)\\end{aligned}]{u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304796 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304541 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304796 1069 "q-328607" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304796 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304796 1071 "Find the polynomial $P(x)$ given that $-3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}-7x+60=-(x+3)(x-4)P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304796 1072 #uuid "0b6781c6-fbc9-5b3d-86e2-c16bb96fea3c" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304796 1074 "Let $f(x)=-3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}-7x+60.$ We know that $(x+3)$ and $(x-4)$ are factors of $f(x),$ and we need to find its other factors.  \n\nBecause $(x+3)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x+3)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division:  \n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-3$ | $-3$ | $4$ | $30$ | $-7$ | $60$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $9$ | $-39$ | $27$ | $-60$ |\n|  | $-3$ | $13$ | $-9$ | $20$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,  \n\n$$\n\\frac{-3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}-7x+60}{x+3}=-3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}-9x+20,\n$$\n\nwhich means that  \n\n$$\n-3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}-7x+60=(x+3)(-3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}-9x+20).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-4)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(-3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}-9x+20).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division once more:  \n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $-3$ | $13$ | $-9$ | $20$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-12$ | $4$ | $-20$ |\n|  | $-3$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,  \n\n$$\n\\frac{-3{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}-9x+20}{x-4}=-3{x}^{2}+x-5,\n$$\n\nwhich means that  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}-7x+60 & =(x+3)(x-4)(-3{x}^{2}+x-5) \\\\  & =-(x+3)(x-4)(3{x}^{2}-x+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see whether the quadratic polynomial $3{x}^{2}-x+5$ can be factored any further, we calculate its discriminant:  \n\n$$\n{b}^{2}-4ac=(-1)^{2}-4(3)(5)=-59<0.\n$$\n\nSo, the quadratic polynomial $3{x}^{2}-x+5$ cannot be factored over the real numbers since its discriminant is negative.  \n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by  \n\n$$\n-3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}-7x+60=-(x+3)(x-4){{(3{x}^{2}-x+5)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $P(x)=3{x}^{2}-x+5.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304797 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304693 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304797 1069 "q-32869" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304797 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304797 1071 "If $g(u)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡u,\\,0,\\,\\frac{1}{u}⟩$ and $u(t)={e}^{2t}$, then $(g∘u)^{′}(t)=$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304797 1072 #uuid "53be4820-056b-50b8-b65a-84c0096afdb4" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304797 1074 "By the chain rule, we have \n\n$$\n(g∘u)^{′}(t)=g′(u(t))u′(t)\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(u) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}0,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\frac{1}{u})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{u},\\,0,\\,-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})=2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g∘u)^{′}(t) & =g′(u(t))u′(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{u(t)},\\,0,\\,-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}(t)}⟩{u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}⟨\\frac{1}{{e}^{2t}},\\,0,\\,-\\frac{1}{({e}^{2t})^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2{e}^{2t}}{{e}^{2t}},\\,0,\\,-\\frac{2{e}^{2t}}{{e}^{4t}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,0,\\,-2{e}^{-2t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304798 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304470 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304798 1069 "q-3289" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304798 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304798 1071 "During the winter in Pasadena, the temperature, in ${F}^{∘}$, $t$ hours after $9$ a.m. can be modeled by the function $T(t)=50+14\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{12}).$ Find the average temperature during the period from $9$ a.m. to $9$ p.m." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304798 1072 #uuid "2e55b4cb-6c56-5cac-893d-482f84ce6bec" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304798 1074 "Since the function is valid starting from $9$ A.M., we can conclude our interval is $[0,12].$\n\nApplying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12-0}{\\int }_{0}^{12}(50+14\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{12}))\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{12}(50t-\\frac{168}{\\pi }\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{12}))|}_{0}^{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[(50\\cdot 12-\\frac{168}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi \\cdot 12}{12}))-(50\\cdot 0-\\frac{168}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi \\cdot 0}{12}))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[(600-\\frac{168}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-(0-\\frac{168}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[(600-\\frac{168}{\\pi }\\cdot (-1))-(-\\frac{168}{\\pi }\\cdot 1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\frac{600\\pi +168}{\\pi }+\\frac{168}{\\pi }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(\\frac{600\\pi +336}{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{600\\pi +336}{12\\pi } \\\\  & ≈{58.9}^{∘}F.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304799 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304101 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304799 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304111 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304799 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304423 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304799 1069 "q-329163" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304799 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304799 1071 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{8-2x}{|x-1|-1}\\ge 0,\\,x>3.\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x\\le $\n{{field-3}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304799 1072 #uuid "29a0184c-569d-5858-8cd7-3b42f29d085e" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304799 1074 "Since $x>3,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >3 \\\\ x-1 & >2 \\\\ |x-1| & >2 \\\\ |x-1|-1 & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $|x-1|-1$ is positive for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x>3).$ So, we can multiply both sides by $|x-1|-1,$ and solve the resulting inequality as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8-2x}{|x-1|-1} & \\ge 0 \\\\ (|x-1|-1)\\cdot \\frac{8-2x}{|x-1|-1} & \\ge (|x-1|-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 8-2x & \\ge 0 \\\\ -2x & \\ge -8 \\\\ x & \\le 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign of the inequality again since we divided by a negative number ($-2$).\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x>3$ and $x\\le 4.$ So, the solution to our inequality is\n\n$$\n3<x\\le 4.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304800 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304397 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304800 1069 "q-32920" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304800 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304800 1071 "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve  $r(t)=\\sqrt{1+t}\\,i+({t}^{2}-5)\\,j+(3t-1)\\,k$ at the point $P(2,4,8),$  valid for all real values of $u?$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304800 1072 #uuid "5b7142e6-92e9-59b8-89fe-aeb635f9b8e8" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304800 1074 "The point $P(2,4,8)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r({t}_{0})+u\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{0}$ corresponds to the value of $t$ at the point $P.$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i+4\\,j+8\\,k \\\\ \\sqrt{1+t}\\,i+({t}^{2}-5)\\,j+(3t-1)\\,k & =2\\,i+4\\,j+8\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+t}=2 \\\\ {t}^{2}-5=4 \\\\ 3t-1=8\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the third equation, we get $t=3.$ This value also satifies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,4,8)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(3).$  \n\nWe calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{1+t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-5)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-1)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1+t}}\\,i+2t\\,j+3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(3) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1+(3)}}\\,i+2(3)\\,j+3\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4}}\\,i+2\\cdot 3\\,j+3\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,i+6\\,j+3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P(2,4,8)$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=2i+4\\,j+8\\,k+u(\\frac{1}{4}\\,i+6\\,j+3\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304801 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304116 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304801 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304647 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304801 1069 "q-329275" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304801 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304801 1071 "Express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{5\\pi }{4})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{5\\pi }{4})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304801 1072 #uuid "7047722e-4f23-5bf4-be44-e612b69308d2" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304801 1074 "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304802 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304366 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304802 1069 "q-32936" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304802 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304802 1071 "Find the parametric equations of the line $\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y-1}{4}=\\frac{z+1}{3}.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304802 1072 #uuid "cf2db5ae-81e4-5762-b815-c025437def46" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304802 1074 "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y-1}{4}=\\frac{z-(-1)}{3},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(2,1,-1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨2,4,3⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2+2t \\\\ y=1+4t \\\\ z=-1+3t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304803 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304601 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304803 1069 "q-3295" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304803 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304803 1071 "Find the average value of the function $f(x)={cos}^{4}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ on the interval $[0,\\pi ].$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304803 1072 #uuid "ecd43ed3-12b1-5649-a96a-79cb243b0602" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304803 1074 "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi -0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{4}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate this integral using a substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{4}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{\\frac{1}{\\pi }(\\frac{{u}^{5}}{5})|}_{1}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5\\pi }[(-1)^{5}-{1}^{5}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5\\pi }(-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304804 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304475 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304804 1069 "q-329515" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304804 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304804 1071 "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(6x+{12}^{∘})=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe general solution of this equation is\n\n$$\nx={8}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}\\cdot n,\\,x=a+{60}^{∘}\\cdot n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $a$ in the range $[{0}^{∘},{60}^{∘}).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304804 1072 #uuid "b2fa30ca-8686-5c21-a334-93076d30e70e" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304804 1074 "First, note that the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x+{12}^{∘})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n6x+{12}^{∘}={60}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n,\\,6x+{12}^{∘}={120}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x & ={60}^{∘}-{12}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\,6x & ={120}^{∘}-{12}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ 6x & ={48}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\,6x & ={108}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ x & ={8}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\,x & ={18}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}\\cdot n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a={18}^{∘}.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304805 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304648 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304805 1069 "q-329544" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304805 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304805 1071 "Consider the following trigonometric equation:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=a\\pi +4\\pi n,\\,x=-\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+4\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $a$ in the range $(-4,0].$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304805 1072 #uuid "70572e92-3315-5d8b-8fa2-73e029201cb6" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304805 1074 "First, note that the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n,\\,\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2\\pi n.\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2} & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n, & \\, & \\frac{x}{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =-\\frac{11\\pi }{12}+2\\pi n, & \\, & \\frac{x}{2}=-\\frac{7\\pi }{12}+2\\pi n \\\\ x & =-\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+4\\pi n, & \\, & x=-\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+4\\pi n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $a=-\\frac{11}{6}.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304806 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304091 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304806 1069 "q-3297" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304806 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304806 1071 "Within  the interval $[0,\\pi ]$, determine the average value of $f(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304806 1072 #uuid "24f38454-bd1c-55d7-83bd-a807c36fe3b4" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304806 1074 "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{avg}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi -0}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{\\pi }(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }[(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\pi ))-(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }[(-2\\cdot (-1)+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1)-(-2\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }[(\\frac{5}{2})-(-\\frac{3}{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(4) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304807 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304437 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304807 1069 "q-3298" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304807 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304807 1071 "Find ${({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$  as a function of ${g}^{-1}(x),$ if $g(x)=8{x}^{2}+x.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304807 1072 #uuid "c1a520cf-96ad-5105-badd-6e9dbbb27525" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304807 1074 "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=8{x}^{2}+x,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=8{y}^{2}+y\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={g}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(8{y}^{2}+y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =16y+1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{16y+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${g}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={g}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{g}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{16{g}^{-1}(x)+1} \\\\ {({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{16{g}^{-1}(x)+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304808 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304361 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304808 1069 "q-3300" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304808 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304808 1071 "Find ${({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$  as a function of ${g}^{-1}(x),$ if $g(x)=4{x}^{3}+2x+6.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304808 1072 #uuid "54994de7-137e-54bf-80bb-ec8f86707073" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304808 1074 "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=4{x}^{3}+2x+6,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=4{y}^{3}+2y+6\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={g}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(4{y}^{3}+2y+6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =12{y}^{2}+2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{12{y}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${g}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={g}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{g}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2} \\\\ {({g}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{12({g}^{-1}(x))^{2}+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304809 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304616 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304809 1069 "q-33002" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304809 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304809 1071 "Consider the straight line with the canonical equation $\\frac{x-3}{5}=\\frac{y-1}{1}=\\frac{z+7}{-5}.$ Find the vector equation of the line." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304809 1072 #uuid "9d273191-2166-59ea-847e-321a4a853467" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304809 1074 "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{5}=\\frac{y-1}{1}=\\frac{z-(-7)}{-5},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(3,1,-7)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨5,1,-5⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304810 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304649 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304810 1069 "q-33006" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304810 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304810 1071 "Consider the straight line with the parametric equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2+4t \\\\ y=5+5t \\\\ z=-1+6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304810 1072 #uuid "5bff15f3-7d00-5ef9-bbfd-b3b1a0eabb82" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304810 1074 "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2+4t \\\\ 5+5t \\\\ -1+6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 5t \\\\ 6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(2,5,-1)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨4,5,6⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y-5}{5}=\\frac{z-(-1)}{6}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{y-5}{5}=\\frac{z+1}{6}.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304811 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304449 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304811 1069 "q-3301" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304811 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304811 1071 "If $f(x)=12x+5,$ find ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${f}^{-1}(x).$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304811 1072 #uuid "b2f63846-6110-5517-8746-331a837cdaa1" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304811 1074 "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=12x+5,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=12y+5\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(12y+5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${f}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{f}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{12} \\\\ {({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304812 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304152 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304812 1069 "q-3302" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304812 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304812 1071 "If $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+9,$ find ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x)$ as a function of ${f}^{-1}(x).$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304812 1072 #uuid "b19970c2-81b2-5ca3-b128-2b4613b17aae" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304812 1074 "**Step 1:** First, we write the inverse function implicitly. The given function is $y=2{x}^{2}+9,$ and to write the inverse function implicitly, we swap $x$ and $y$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=2{y}^{2}+9\n$$\n\nNote that in the equation above, we have $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\n**Step 2:** Next, we differentiate. The overall goal is to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ but the equation is already solved for $x,$ so we compute $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y}$ and then take the reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{2}+9) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =4y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{4y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Lastly, we write the result in terms of ${f}^{-1}(x).$ Since $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[{f}^{-1}(x)] & =\\frac{1}{4{f}^{-1}(x)} \\\\ {({f}^{-1})}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{4{f}^{-1}(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304813 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304259 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304813 1069 "q-330218" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304813 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304813 1071 "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+{120}^{∘})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as\n\n$$\nx=-{45}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n,\\,x=a+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $a$ in the range $[{0}^{∘},{180}^{∘}).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304813 1072 #uuid "9d99737b-6bbd-5e8c-8334-59eb5fdcaa0b" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304813 1074 "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+{120}^{∘} & ={30}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\, & \\, & 2x+{120}^{∘} & ={330}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={30}^{∘}-{120}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & 2x & ={330}^{∘}-{120}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ 2x & =-{90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & 2x & ={210}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ x & =-{45}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & x & ={105}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}\\cdot n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a={105}^{∘}.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304814 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304357 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304814 1069 "q-33027" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304814 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304814 1071 "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $Q(1,2,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304814 1072 #uuid "62f6f273-804e-559c-a53a-0d8f68234b8d" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304814 1074 "First, note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $n=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes though the point $Q({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(r-q)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $q$ is the position vector of $Q.$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304815 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304112 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304815 1069 "q-330297" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304815 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304815 1071 "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- $2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$\n- $-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$\n- $3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304815 1072 #uuid "8f297359-1573-550c-acfb-39ada6af0bec" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304815 1074 "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Indeed, notice that the linear combination \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)=2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+1\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ according to the linearity principle. Thus, \n\n$$\n{{2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{h}(t)}+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our inhomogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-5\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $-1$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $3\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)-2\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+3\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $3$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304816 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304613 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304816 1069 "q-330298" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304816 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304816 1071 "Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfy the homogeneous system ${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ satisfy the inhomogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t)+f(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same inhomogeneous system?\n\n- $-2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+4\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$\n- ${x}_{2}(t)-{x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$\n- $-7\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304816 1072 #uuid "78db71f4-3fc9-53f6-9a4c-5f66663f6749" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304816 1074 "Recall that if ${x}_{h}(t)$ satisfies a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution to an inhomogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ then ${x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)$ is also a solution to the same inhomogeneous system.\n\nAdditonally, the *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t),$ ${x}_{2}(t),…,{x}_{k}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ then any linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)+⋯+{c}_{k}{x}_{k}(t)\n$$\n\nwith ${c}_{1},{c}_{2},…,{c}_{k}\\in R$ is also a solution of the same homogeneous system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expression.\n\n- Option I might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that $-2\\cdot {x}_{1}(t)+4\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution of the homogeneous system, because of the multiplier $4$ next to the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the inhomogeneous system. Notice that ${x}_{2}(t)-{x}_{1}(t)+2\\cdot {x}_{p}(t)$ can't be written as \n\n$$\n{x}_{h}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{h}(t)$ is a solution 