533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304824 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=i-\\sqrt{3}j+4k$ and $n=8i+\\sqrt{27}j$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as \n\n$$\n(r-p)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot (8i+\\sqrt{27}j) & =(i-\\sqrt{3}j+4k)\\cdot (8i+\\sqrt{27}j) \\\\ r\\cdot (8i+\\sqrt{27}j) & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304825 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304385 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304825 1069 "q-330353" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304825 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304825 1071 "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{′}(t)=A\\text{ }x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)⊙{x}_{2}(t)$ (element-wise product)" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304825 1072 #uuid "89894e08-2da2-5128-a91b-b1054d28b3f7" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304825 1074 "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier of the second term is not a constant.\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304826 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304196 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304826 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304636 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304826 1069 "q-330397" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304826 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304826 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the exact solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2}=0,$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$  are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304826 1072 #uuid "cd2ab0e4-e20f-5959-8e4d-1d88f775ade3" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304826 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{2} & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is not in the range $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ To get the first solution that's within the required range, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ To do this, we simply add $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2\\pi =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](images/01/012f0986293e2a4318dc68633a4ac0e91732d74300496427aaaa6635b417efe4.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order,  the solutions are \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,x=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304827 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304734 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304827 1069 "q-3304" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304827 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304827 1071 "Find the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${8}^{x}\\cdot {4}^{2x-1}=32.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304827 1072 #uuid "62eb03df-7254-5fcb-be19-35dd658881b9" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304827 1074 "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $8={2}^{3}$, $4={2}^{2}$ and $32={2}^{5},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({2}^{3})}^{x}\\cdot {({2}^{2})}^{2x-1}={2}^{5}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule and the product rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{3x}\\cdot {2}^{2(2x-1)} & ={2}^{5} \\\\ {2}^{3x+2(2x-1)} & ={2}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(2)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2(2x-1) & =5 \\\\ 3x+4x-2 & =5 \\\\ 7x & =7 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304828 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304380 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304828 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304499 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304828 1069 "q-330495" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304828 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304828 1071 "In **ascending order**, rounded to two decimal places in radians, the solutions to the equation $3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ are\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304828 1072 #uuid "83f44b93-f5ec-58b1-808e-e00dc358b006" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304828 1074 "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{8\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\ 3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =8 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{8}{3})≈1.212\\,03\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places. \n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\nx≈1.212\\,03+\\pi ≈4.353\\,62\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places. \n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to two decimal places, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}≈1.21,\\,{x}_{2}≈4.35.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304201 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304829 1069 "q-330498" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304829 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304829 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,-2,4)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=⟨0,3,-5⟩.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304829 1072 #uuid "00e129f5-c1b2-5333-8f70-babdfeae2e75" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304829 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨1,-2,4⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨0,3,-5⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find an equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ & =⟨1,-2,4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,3,-5⟩ & =-26 \\\\ 3y-5z & =-26 \\\\ 3y-5z+26 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304830 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304190 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304830 1069 "q-330500" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304830 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304830 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,1,-5)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=⟨6,-2,0⟩.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304830 1072 #uuid "03f5292d-2cbb-5362-966c-d91bc52e2d79" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304830 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨2,1,-5⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨6,-2,0⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find an equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨6,-2,0⟩ & =⟨2,1,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨6,-2,0⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨6,-2,0⟩ & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨6,-2,0⟩ & =10 \\\\ 6x-2y & =10 \\\\ 6x-2y-10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304831 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304135 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304831 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304721 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304831 1069 "q-330590" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304831 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304831 1071 "In **ascending order**, rounded to two decimal places in radians, the solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ are\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304831 1072 #uuid "ea746fcc-7b19-5ba3-9c75-5b1b4e610dfc" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304831 1074 "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ 2\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =5 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{2})≈1.190\\,29\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places. \n\nTo find another solution in the interval $[-\\pi ,\\pi )$, we subtract $\\pi :$\n\n$$\nx≈1.190\\,29-\\pi ≈-1.951\\,30\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places. \n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to two decimal places, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}≈-1.95,\\,{x}_{2}≈1.19.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304832 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304261 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304832 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304591 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304832 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304761 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304832 1069 "q-330599" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304832 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304832 1071 "Solve the inequality\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt{x+1}-3)(x-2)\\le 0,\\,x<8.\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x<$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304832 1072 #uuid "a4888a0f-1e1b-58c8-bcef-851b77db5f5e" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304832 1074 "Since $x<8,$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & <8 \\\\ x+1 & <9 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+1} & <3 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+1}-3 & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\sqrt{x+1}-3$ is negative for all the values of $x$ that we are considering $(x<8).$ So, we can divide both sides by $\\sqrt{x+1}-3,$ provided that we flip the sign of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{x+1}-3)(x-2) & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{(\\sqrt{x+1}-3)(x-2)}{\\sqrt{x+1}-3} & \\ge \\frac{0}{\\sqrt{x+1}-3} \\\\ x-2 & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the inequality will be the numbers that satisfy both $x\\ge 2$ and $x<8.$ So, the solution to our inequality is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\le x<8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304833 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304540 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304833 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304686 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304833 1069 "q-330609" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304833 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304833 1071 "Find the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304833 1072 #uuid "db8062c6-6958-5892-b549-e7a0aebb6682" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304833 1074 "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies in the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add or subtract integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =(-2)\\cdot \\pi +x=-\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304834 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304489 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304834 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304618 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304834 1069 "q-330627" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304834 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304834 1071 "In **ascending order**, rounded to two decimal places in radians, the solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0,$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ are\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304834 1072 #uuid "8e9f6a67-9215-59b5-9e73-fd42ec4032af" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304834 1074 "We divide each term of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{7\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{7\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-7 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =7 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{7}{2})≈1.292\\,50\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places.\n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}≈1.292\\,50+\\pi ≈4.434\\,09\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places.\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to two decimal places, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}≈1.29,\\,{x}_{2}≈4.43.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304835 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304736 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304835 1069 "q-33063" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304835 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304835 1071 "What is the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k$ at the point $P(2\\pi ,1,0)?$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304835 1072 #uuid "cc93a8dd-5996-5f2c-b3c8-d030c2d30016" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304835 1074 "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,j+y(t)\\,j+z(t)\\,k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by \n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(2\\pi ,1,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\pi \\,i+\\,j+0\\,k \\\\ t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k & =2\\pi \\,i+\\,j+0\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t=2\\pi  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t=1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=2\\pi .$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, the point $P(2\\pi ,1,0)$ is at the tip of $r(2\\pi ).$  \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2\\pi :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(2\\pi ) & =\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )\\,k \\\\  & =i+0\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =i+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(2\\pi ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(2\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+0+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(2\\pi )$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(2\\pi ,1,0):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2\\pi ) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(2\\pi )}{‖{r}^{′}(2\\pi )‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot (i+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304836 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304535 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304836 1069 "q-330631" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304836 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304836 1071 "| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3783\\,$ | $\\,0.3745\\,$ | $\\,0.3707\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4168\\,$ | $\\,0.4129\\,$ | $\\,0.4090\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.4562\\,$ | $\\,0.4522\\,$ | $\\,0.4483\\,$ |\n\nThe data above is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ compute $P(Z>0.33).$ Use the table above and do not round your answer.\n\n$P(Z>0.33)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304836 1072 #uuid "57e3fa8d-be80-5950-ac96-0406b71dc7f1" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304836 1074 "![](images/59/597125714057269b7adf9c0a2a9bbdde9631cf37bc80518281ab4444c