|\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6443\\,$ | $\\,0.6480\\,$ | $\\,0.6517\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6808\\,$ | $\\,0.6844\\,$ | $\\,0.6879\\,$ |\n\n$P(Z<-0.38)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304841 1072 #uuid "95b34f12-173d-5a57-a061-ad312a9c74ac" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304841 1074 "![](images/8a/8a6467813ccf982906fb5d1d8cd5c5c88b984c22a1e238715c65315a7772249c.png)\n\nFor a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ we have the following symmetry property for $a>0:$\n\n$$\nP(Z>a)=P(Z<-a)=Φ(-a)\n$$\n\nWe're given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(Z<-0.38).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/ee/ee2aebec9953a09dca6eab5c7e5434240885a383a6701638fffdaa9ee9cfeb40.png)\n\nBy symmetry, this equals the area corresponding to the probability $P(Z>0.38).$\n\n![](images/61/619fae52bde8b2da729095f733e1ea1411132fffe70cc23e252a83cbb2e7ff23.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z<-0.38) & =P(Z>0.38) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.38) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.38) \\\\  & =1-0.6480 \\\\  & =0.3520.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304842 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304203 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304842 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304331 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304842 1069 "q-330663" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304842 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304842 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{5}{8},$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}, $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304842 1072 #uuid "74363503-2307-56fc-b6a7-986e093312fa" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304842 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{5}{8})=0.675\\,131…≈0.6751\n$$\n\nSince $0.675\\,1$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.6751.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.6751.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.6751.$\n\n![](images/cc/cc57a94c1f4f4fa53e35930c053b8f2e3f2be0fee0beb8d1584835a0f134b800.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -0.675\\,1 \\\\  & ≈2.4665\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈0.7,\\,x≈2.5.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304843 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304688 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304843 1069 "q-33067" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304843 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304843 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(5,0,-3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=⟨2,4,-1⟩.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304843 1072 #uuid "972806e5-5276-519b-b09e-86229dab2cf0" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304843 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨5,0,-3⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨2,4,-1⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find an equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,4,-1⟩ & =⟨5,0,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,4,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,4,-1⟩ & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨2,4,-1⟩ & =13 \\\\ 2x+4y-z & =13 \\\\ 2x+4y-z-13 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304844 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304509 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304844 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304572 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304844 1069 "q-330672" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304844 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304844 1071 "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-1=-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+13$ for $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order**, the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304844 1072 #uuid "fa607826-5ea4-5818-9f6a-beb379e9ace2" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304844 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-1 & =-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+13 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =14 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2}{7})=0.278\\,2…≈0.278\n$$\n\nSince $0.278$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution, rounded to one decimal place, is ${x}_{1}=0.3.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add and subtract integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\pi +x=-\\pi +0.278≈-2.9,\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-2.9$ and $x=0.3.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304845 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304634 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304845 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304758 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304845 1069 "q-330688" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304845 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304845 1071 "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to the nearest integer**, the two solutions to the equation $4\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+6$ for $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<{180}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304845 1072 #uuid "1f6f8a2c-40c9-5d87-a727-71eabdc1a083" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304845 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+6 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =6 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=(26.565\\,0…)^{∘}≈{26.565}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${26.565}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution, rounded to the nearest integer, is ${x}_{1}={27}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-{180}^{∘}+x=-{180}^{∘}+{26.565}^{∘}≈-{153}^{∘},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x={-153}^{∘}$ and $x={27}^{∘}.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304846 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304270 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304846 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304517 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304846 1069 "q-330707" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304846 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304846 1071 "Find the two solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7$ for $0<x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304846 1072 #uuid "e7aae93e-00cb-501a-8a72-2134e4ceeb78" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304846 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7 & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304847 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304179 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304847 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304327 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304847 1069 "q-330708" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304847 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304847 1071 "Find the two solutions to the equation $\\sqrt{8-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}=\\sqrt{8}$ for $0<x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304847 1072 #uuid "7f1dcf48-0ac1-55e5-8958-73e963ab1c97" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304847 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{8-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\ 8-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =8 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304848 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304404 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304848 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304407 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304848 1069 "q-330722" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304848 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304848 1071 "Find the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\sqrt{18}=3\\sqrt{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}<x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304848 1072 #uuid "9c4b77ca-a7cf-5441-ba29-4c8b5dfbf25f" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304848 1074 "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\sqrt{18} & =3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =3\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{18} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =x+{180}^{∘}={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}={270}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $x={90}^{∘}$ and $x={270}^{∘}.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304849 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304222 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304849 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304330 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304849 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304552 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304849 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304754 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304849 1069 "q-330741" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304849 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304849 1071 "The second-order differential equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+3y={e}^{t}$ can be expressed as the matrix equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t),$ where\n\n| $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,A=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,,\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-3$ | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |\n\nand for $y=y(t),$ we have $y={x}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}^{′}={x}_{2}(t).$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304849 1072 #uuid "1960dd8a-671e-56d5-ad40-99d332ab17cf" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304849 1074 "We first need to express the given differential equation as a system of first-order differential equations. Then, we write the system as a matrix equation.\n\nWe start by making the following change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =y \\\\ {x}_{2} & ={y}^{′}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={y}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2}, \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}={y}^{″}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now write the right-hand side of the last equation in terms of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}.$\n\nSolving our original differential equation for the highest-order derivative $({y}^{″}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=2{y}^{′}-3y+{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variables into the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=2{x}_{2}-3{x}_{1}+{e}^{t}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not include any derivative terms on the right-hand side. \n\nTherefore, our complete system of equations is as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}={x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}=-3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can represent this system as the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+f(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′} \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304180 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304237 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304850 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304247 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17