\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f(t)$ at $t=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =4u(6-6) \\\\  & =4u(0) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(6)=4.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304902 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304106 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304902 1069 "q-331242" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304902 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304902 1071 "Evaluate $f(t)=5u(t-8)$ at $t=8,$ where $u$ is the unit step function.\n\n$f(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304902 1072 #uuid "1e673c48-7462-5f02-a3fb-dcb04097a082" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304902 1074 "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating $f(t)$ at $t=8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(8) & =5u(8-8) \\\\  & =5u(0) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(8)=5.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304903 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304713 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304903 1069 "q-331246" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304903 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304903 1071 "![](images/c3/c3d512c9e89079d86c29d1aaa8feb9fa8a47345fda4a1c0e2f2f5228246d0ef5.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304903 1072 #uuid "a632fd40-1754-5cbe-adc5-e4166b2bb498" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304903 1074 "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9fb8c20672705e9d68e69d32e7ff3499ec71560e10aa85136b3f3ad75c5fb5ed.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we shift $y=u(t)$ by $2$ units to the left, giving $y=u(t+2).$\n\n- There is no vertical scaling or offset.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=u(t+2).$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304904 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304230 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304904 1069 "q-331247" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304904 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304904 1071 "![](images/d8/d82e49514527a4f46f7bb212f0169806eb1475dd5324dda88ab3d733521540a9.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304904 1072 #uuid "1e5fc780-02ea-5c04-9ccc-da5815e936fb" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304904 1074 "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9fb8c20672705e9d68e69d32e7ff3499ec71560e10aa85136b3f3ad75c5fb5ed.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we shift $y=u(t)$ by $1$ unit to the right, giving $y=u(t-1).$\n\n- There is no vertical scaling or offset.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=u(t-1).$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304905 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304386 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304905 1069 "q-331248" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304905 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304905 1071 "![](images/64/64dffc57707b89d61185a1158359c0dce3e4ded8d7752886336da7154f6b86ec.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304905 1072 #uuid "ca4f26a2-7fbf-5d14-bce3-ec481ea9d409" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304905 1074 "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9fb8c20672705e9d68e69d32e7ff3499ec71560e10aa85136b3f3ad75c5fb5ed.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we shift $y=u(t)$ by $3$ units to the left, giving $y=u(t+3).$\n\n- There is no vertical scaling or offset.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=u(t+3).$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304906 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304743 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304906 1069 "q-331267" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304906 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304906 1071 "![](images/c3/c3680f88195487cb34a0c288dc605bbd731868433f52317a7cd31797caad3dd2.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304906 1072 #uuid "0a2a61e0-a704-5fe3-a9cb-1c77ed1f9e58" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304906 1074 "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/a1/a1748c6e23d99ad6ef43d5aceedcadb7a12fcdbef5064f93202d2fe5ca3d98d7.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y=u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y=u(-t).$\n\n- Next, we shift by one unit to the right, giving $y=u(-(t-1))=u(1-t).$\n\n- There is no vertical scaling or offset.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=u(1-t).$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304907 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304083 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304907 1069 "q-331268" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304907 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304907 1071 "![](images/50/50c72e6334bdf9638b7915ef96157f871310faf6c9d3b7bfc4d4f8c7f65c9d26.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(t)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be $f(t),$ where $u$ denotes the unit step function?" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304907 1072 #uuid "1d0077c9-39bb-5774-b060-7a38064de5a4" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304907 1074 "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/a1/a1748c6e23d99ad6ef43d5aceedcadb7a12fcdbef5064f93202d2fe5ca3d98d7.png)\n\nNotice that to transform $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y=u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y=u(-t).$\n\n- Next, we shift by one unit to the left, giving $y=u(-(t+1))=u(-1-t).$\n\n- There is no vertical scaling or offset.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=u(-1-t).$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304908 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304516 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304908 1069 "q-33130" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304908 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304908 1071 "What is the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+2{t}^{2}\\,k$ at the point $P(2,4,8)?$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304908 1072 #uuid "887f8389-6623-5108-b006-b14ec31e2d24" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304908 1074 "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,j+y(t)\\,j+z(t)\\,k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(2,4,8)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i+4\\,j+8\\,k \\\\ t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+2{t}^{2}\\,k & =2\\,i+4\\,j+8\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t=2 \\\\ {t}^{2}=4 \\\\ 2{t}^{2}=8.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=2.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, the point $P(2,4,8)$ is at the tip of $r(2).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j+4t\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(2) & =\\,i+2\\cdot 2\\,j+4\\cdot 2\\,k \\\\  & =i+4\\,j+8\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(2)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{8}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{81} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(2)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(2,4,8):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(2)}{‖{r}^{′}(2)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (i+4\\,j+8\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\,i+\\frac{4}{9}\\,j+\\frac{8}{9}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304909 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304440 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304909 1069 "q-33133" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304909 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304909 1071 "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $D(-6,3,2)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=⟨4,2,-1⟩$  and ${v}_{2}=⟨4,-3,2⟩.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304909 1072 #uuid "9271c80c-c992-52e7-8ded-e69bd152c1f5" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304909 1074 "A parametric equation of a plane is given by \n\n$$\nr=p+sb+tc,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $b$ and $c$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nHere, the position vector of the given point $D$ is $p=⟨-6,3,2⟩$ and the two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other) are ${v}_{1}=⟨4,2,-1⟩$  and ${v}_{2}=⟨4,-3,2⟩.$\n\nSo, a parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨-6,3,2⟩+s⟨4,2,-1⟩+t⟨4,-3,2⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304910 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304394 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304910 1069 "q-33135" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304910 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304910 1071 "What is the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨4t,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩$ at the point $P(4,0,1)?$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304910 1072 #uuid "3cf5589b-b0ad-5c9f-91a0-818d6183c44f" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304910 1074 "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(4,0,1)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨4,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨4t,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩ & =⟨4,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4t=4 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t=0 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, the point $P(4,0,1)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$  \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,\\frac{4}{t},\\,2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨4,\\,\\frac{4}{(1)},\\,2(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,4,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(4,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨4,\\,4,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304911 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304587 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304911 1069 "q-33136" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304911 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304911 1071 "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{t}^{2}⟩$ at the point where $t=\\pi .$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304911 1072 #uuid "ed58ae3d-4e2e-509f-b710-dac0bd55a1aa" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304911 1074 "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\pi :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{3}t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\pi ,\\,0,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(\\pi ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{(-\\pi )^{2}+(0)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3}\\pi )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{\\pi }^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\pi ) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(\\pi )}{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }⟨-\\pi ,\\,0,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{2},\\,0,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304912 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304398 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304912 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304493 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304912 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304576 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304912 1069 "q-331373" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304912 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304912 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2},$ fill the missing entries in the table below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304912 1072 #uuid "6fd7e8a3-5fa7-5932-ac94-21c23a621d39" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304912 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe predictor equation of the modified Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p}={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe initially predicted new $y$-value is then given by ${y}_{p}=y+Δ{y}_{p}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={0}^{2}+2\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n