$y$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x$ and $Δy$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{7}{16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{1}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{2}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,{y}_{3}^{′}\\,$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{7}{16}$ |\n| $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{9}{16}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\,(-\\frac{1}{2})≈-\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304918 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304769 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304918 1069 "q-33142" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304918 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304918 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(2,-5,1)$, $P(4,-2,3)$ and $Q(5,1,0).$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304918 1072 #uuid "0886b819-4519-5617-bf4c-2f8309f1feb4" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304918 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨2,-5,1⟩,\\,p=⟨4,-2,3⟩,$ and $q=⟨5,1,0⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨4,-2,3⟩-⟨2,-5,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3,2⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨5,1,0⟩-⟨2,-5,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,6,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-15i+8j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-15,8,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =⟨4,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =-67\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =-67 \\\\ -15x+8y+3z & =-67 \\\\ -15x+8y+3z+67 & =0 \\\\ 15x-8y-3z-67 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304919 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304722 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304919 1069 "q-331458" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304919 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304919 1071 "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{5t}-{e}^{2t}$ for $s>5.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304919 1072 #uuid "830ad22d-fba6-59df-a5a5-a21ecd67d0c3" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304919 1074 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{5t}-{e}^{2t}} & =L{{e}^{5t}}+L{-{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{5t}}-L{{e}^{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{5t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-5},\\,s>5 \\\\ L{{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-2},\\,s>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{5t}}-L{{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-5}-\\frac{1}{s-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-2)-(s-5)}{(s-5)(s-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{s-2-s+5}{(s-5)(s-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(s-5)(s-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{5t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (5,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{2t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (2,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(5,\\infty )\\cap (2,\\infty )=(5,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>5.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304920 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304220 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304920 1069 "q-331459" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304920 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304920 1071 "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-3t}-{e}^{t}$ for $s>1.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304920 1072 #uuid "96031e76-c06e-54a9-9f6e-9b6bb0b4d0fa" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304920 1074 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-3t}-{e}^{t}} & =L{{e}^{-3t}}+L{-{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{-3t}}-L{{e}^{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-3t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+3},\\,s>-3 \\\\ L{{e}^{t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-1},\\,s>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{-3t}}-L{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+3}-\\frac{1}{s-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-1)-(s+3)}{(s+3)(s-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{(s+3)(s-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{(s+3)(s-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{-3t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-3,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (1,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(-3,\\infty )\\cap (1,\\infty )=(1,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>1.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304921 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304224 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304921 1069 "q-331462" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304921 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304921 1071 "A biased spinner has a $60%$ chance of landing on blue. The spinner is spun $4$ times. Rounded to three decimal places, what is the probability of getting blue exactly $2$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304921 1072 #uuid "32f944b1-b534-5901-8e9b-d8d2c9973f2e" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304921 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the spinner landed on blue.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=4$ spins.\n\n- The probability of landing on blue is $p=60%=0.6.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(4,0.6).$ We want to find the probability of landing on blue $x=2$ times.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2})(0.6)^{2}(1-0.6)^{4-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4!}{2!(4-2)!}(0.6)^{2}(0.4)^{2} \\\\  & =6(0.6)^{2}(0.4)^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.346,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304922 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304477 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304922 1069 "q-331463" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304922 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304922 1071 "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=3-5{e}^{2t}$ for $s>2.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304922 1072 #uuid "4407f5ea-038b-59e3-a845-79ee46ad42dd" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304922 1074 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{3-5{e}^{2t}} & =L{3}+L{-5{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =3L{1}-5L{{e}^{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\n$$\nL{1}=\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-2},\\,s>2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =3L{1}-5L{{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{s}-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{s}-\\frac{5}{s-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(s-2)-5s}{s(s-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(s+3)}{s(s-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{1}$ is defined for $s\\in (0,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{2t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (2,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(0,\\infty )\\cap (2,\\infty )=(2,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>2.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304923 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304278 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304923 1069 "q-331464" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304923 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304923 1071 "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=1-3{e}^{4t}$ for $s>4.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304923 1072 #uuid "3c58cf08-7484-5ffe-b119-69ffe9002513" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304923 1074 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1-3{e}^{4t}} & =L{1}+L{-3{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =L{1}-3L{{e}^{4t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k\n$$\n\n$$\nL{1}=\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{e}^{4t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-4},\\,s>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{1}-3L{{e}^{4t}} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{s}-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}-\\frac{3}{s-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-4)-3s}{s(s-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(s+2)}{s(s-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{1}$ is defined for $s\\in (0,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{5t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (5,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{f(t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(0,\\infty )\\cap (4,\\infty )=(4,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>4.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304924 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304574 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304924 1069 "q-331494" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304924 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304924 1071 "A fair eight-sided die is rolled $6$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability of getting a $7$ or $8$ exactly two times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304924 1072 #uuid "e7511a65-5c20-5b44-9ad2-de1b2ee180fa" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304924 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times that a $7$ or $8$ is rolled. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=6$ rolls.\n\n- The probability of getting a $7$ or $8$ on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{2}{8}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(6,\\frac{1}{4}).$ We want to find the probability of $x=2$ successes.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{2}){(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})}^{6-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6!}{2!(6-2)!}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{4} \\\\  & =15{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{4} \\\\  & ≈0.297,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304925 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304118 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304925 1069 "q-331500" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304925 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304925 1071 "Given that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t$ for $s>0.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304925 1072 #uuid "dab70b23-35c7-51c1-a96d-2f800a666744" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304925 1074 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t} & =L{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}+L{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t} \\\\  & =5L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}-2L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0, \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t} & =\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =5L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}-2L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}-2\\cdot \\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16} \\\\  & =\\frac{5s}{{s}^{2}+16}-\\frac{8}{{s}^{2}+16} \\\\  & =\\frac{5s-8}{{s}^{2}+16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304926 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304724 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304926 1069 "q-331504" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304926 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304926 1071 "Given that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of $f(t)=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t$ for $s>0.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304926 1072 #uuid "31d7aadf-2aca-5f87-8d9d-4dbfa411cc43" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304926 1074 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and