}}^{2}{y}_{p}^{2}-{y}_{p}^{2} \\\\  & =(0)^{2}(1)^{2}-(1)^{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+(-1))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304446 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304703 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304725 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304931 1069 "q-331560" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304931 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304931 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the modified Euler method. Complete the first row of the table below, given that the step size is $Δx=1.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304931 1072 #uuid "7df29ffb-0f42-5d81-8c3a-a74f11556867" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304931 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=1-1=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $0$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p}) \\\\  & ={x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+{y}_{p}^{2}-2 \\\\  & =(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $-1$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-1+(-1))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-2) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $-1$ |" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304932 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304239 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304932 1069 "q-33160" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304932 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304932 1071 "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $C(1,4,-14)$ and is parallel to the two distinct lines $r=⟨-1,5,7⟩+s⟨1,1,1⟩$ and $r=⟨0,3,-19⟩+t⟨-2,-2,-2⟩.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304932 1072 #uuid "133e1de3-de48-5298-8948-a57726f1d155" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304932 1074 "The direction vectors of the lines are ${v}_{1}=⟨-2,-2,-2⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨1,1,1⟩,$ respectively.\nBut ${v}_{2}=-2{v}_{1}.$ So, our lines are parallel. \n\nDespite this fact, two distinct parallel lines still determine a plane. Now: \n\n- from the equation of the first line, we get the point $P$ with the position vector $p=⟨-1,5,7⟩$ that lies on it, and\n\n- from the equation of the second line, we get the point $Q$ with the position vector $q=⟨0,3,-19⟩$ that lies on it.\n\nThe vector $u=q-p$ will be parallel to the required plane. \n\nComputing the coordinates of $u,$  we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨0,3,-19⟩-⟨-1,5,7⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-2,-26⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}∦{v}_{1},$ we can find the equation of our plane.\n\nWe know that the point $C(1,4,-14)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $c=⟨1,4,-14⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =c+s{v}_{1}+tu \\\\ r & =⟨1,4,-14⟩+s⟨1,1,1⟩+t⟨1,-2,-26⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304933 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304640 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304933 1069 "q-331605" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304933 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304933 1071 "Which of the following first-order linear differential equations are inhomogeneous?\n\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0$\n- ${y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304933 1072 #uuid "52b69d13-8d12-5e35-b5cc-e11a7f6b65a8" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304933 1074 "A first-order linear differential equation is homogeneous if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nA first-order linear differential equation is inhomogeneous if it is not homogeneous, i.e., if it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $Q$ is not identical to zero.\n\nTo determine which equations are inhomogeneous, we examine each one in turn.\n\n- First, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=3x.\n$$\n\nNotice that this is written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=Q(x)$ where $Q(x)=3x$ is not identically zero. Therefore, this equation is inhomogeneous.\n\n- Next, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation has the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0.$ Therefore, this equation is homogeneous.\n\n- Finally, we consider the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y=y.\n$$\n\nWe collect the $y$-terms on the left side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y & =y \\\\ {y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y-y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′}+({x}^{2}-1)y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our equation can be written in the form ${y}^{′}+P(x)y=0,$ the equation is homogeneous.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304934 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304583 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304934 1069 "q-33162" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304934 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304934 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-3,0,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$r=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304934 1072 #uuid "6438ac3c-8420-5b05-be4d-39c722849ed1" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304934 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨-3,0,4⟩.$ From the vectorial equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨3,-5,2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨3,-5,2⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ & =⟨-3,0,4⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-5,2⟩ & =-1 \\\\ 3x-5y+2z & =-1 \\\\ 3x-5y+2z+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304935 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304629 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304935 1069 "q-33164" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304935 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304935 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-4,3,0)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=2i+5j-8k.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304935 1072 #uuid "6e8a4061-bb52-5786-a928-eac2a59e8597" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304935 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-4,3,0⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨2,5,-8⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,5,-8⟩ & =⟨-4,3,0⟩\\cdot ⟨2,5,-8⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,5,-8⟩ & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨2,5,-8⟩ & =7 \\\\ 2x+5y-8z & =7 \\\\ 2x+5y-8z-7 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304936 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304165 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304936 1069 "q-331645" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304936 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304936 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}}, &  & s>0 \\\\ L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfind the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=-3t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ for $s>0.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304936 1072 #uuid "96ac4cd7-8f76-567f-a125-3fef991f4b78" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304936 1074 "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity properties, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{-3t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =L{-3t}+L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =-3L{t}+L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =-3L{t}+L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{s\\cdot {s}^{2}-3({s}^{2}+1)}{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{3}-3{s}^{2}-3}{{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+1)},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304937 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304295 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304937 1069 "q-331650" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304937 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304937 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x(xy+y),\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse the modified Euler method with one step to approximate $y(2).$\n\n$y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304937 1072 #uuid "da812249-faf8-559c-bda1-2131502449da" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304937 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(xy+y) \\\\  & =1(1\\cdot 1+1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=1+2=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ |  |  |\n\nSo, the predicted slope at this point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={x}_{\\text{new}}({x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{p}+{y}_{p}) \\\\  & =2(2\\cdot 3+3) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $18$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+18)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $18$ | $10$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy=1+10=11.\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $18$ | $10$ |\n| $2$ | $11$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈11.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304938 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304412 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304938 1069 "q-331656" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304938 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304938 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x(2xy+3y),\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse the modified Euler method with one step to approximate $y(2).$\n\n$y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304938 1072 #uuid "56da6b00-afbe-503c-9c1f-88355eea31bf" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304938 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe modified Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{p}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the initially predicted slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we add the initial condition data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x(2xy+3y) \\\\  & =1(2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $5$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $5$ | $5$ |  |  |  |\n\nThe coordinates of the initially predicted new point given by the predictor equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=1+5=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |