rname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)}.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304953 1072 #uuid "050795d3-56ec-5162-9d13-5f770f4a560b" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304953 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{6}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)} & =\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304954 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304455 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304954 1069 "q-331864" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304954 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304954 1071 "Given that $L{t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{2\\omega s}{({s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2})^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L{{e}^{3t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)}.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304954 1072 #uuid "0340d014-2204-565e-b89d-a3cd0b5a0c92" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304954 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL{t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)}=\\frac{2(5)s}{({s}^{2}+{5}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{3t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)} & =\\frac{2(5)(s-3)}{((s-3)^{2}+{5}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10(s-3)}{((s-3)^{2}+25)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304955 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304503 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304955 1069 "q-331869" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304955 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304955 1071 "Given that $L{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}{({s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2})^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L{{e}^{3t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)}.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304955 1072 #uuid "4789ba12-67de-59e0-9844-c29467c94a97" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304955 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-{4}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+{4}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{3t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)} & =\\frac{(s-3)^{2}-{4}^{2}}{((s-3)^{2}+{4}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-3)^{2}-16}{((s-3)^{2}+16)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304956 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304323 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304956 1069 "q-331872" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304956 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304956 1071 "Given that  $L{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-{\\omega }^{2}}{({s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2})^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L{{e}^{4t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)}.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304956 1072 #uuid "e98f8075-941a-5e1c-9a37-f46cd62e24a4" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304956 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)}=\\frac{{s}^{2}-{3}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+{3}^{2})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{4t}\\cdot t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)} & =\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-{3}^{2}}{((s-4)^{2}+{3}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(s-4)^{2}-9}{((s-4)^{2}+9)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304957 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304742 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304957 1069 "q-331877" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304957 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304957 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=3{x}^{2}-2x+5.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304957 1072 #uuid "062945b2-f827-54a2-bc28-b7bb74295eab" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304957 1074 "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=4{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=4\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =4x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{4x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304958 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304436 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304958 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304507 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304958 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304631 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304958 1069 "q-331891" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304958 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304958 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)-4{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{{field-1}}=-{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ {{field-2}}={{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304958 1072 #uuid "4e0bd92f-4a0a-5ab6-820a-156e7bb3bb1e" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304958 1074 "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}L{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)}=L{-{x}_{1}(t)} \\\\ L{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)}=L{-3{x}_{1}(t)-4{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}sL{{x}_{1}(t)}-{x}_{1}(0)=-L{{x}_{1}(t)} \\\\ sL{{x}_{2}(t)}-{x}_{2}(0)=-3L{{x}_{1}(t)}-4L{{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-2=-{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-7=-3{X}_{1}(s)-4{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304260 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304389 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304959 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304441 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304959 1069 "q-331892" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304959 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304959 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{{field-1}}=5{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ {{field-2}}={{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304959 1072 #uuid "db364ca4-5215-5fbd-a22a-fdc5895146a8" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304959 1074 "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}L{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)}=L{5{x}_{1}(t)} \\\\ L{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)}=L{{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}sL{{x}_{1}(t)}-{x}_{1}(0)=5L{{x}_{1}(t)} \\\\ sL{{x}_{2}(t)}-{x}_{2}(0)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}-2L{{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+3=5{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+4={X}_{1}(s)-2{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304960 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304102 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304960 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304322 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304960 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304597 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304960 1069 "q-331905" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304960 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304960 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{{field-1}}={{field-2}} \\\\ {{field-3}}=-3{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304960 1072 #uuid "1590ce76-7fce-5848-bda2-73bb0da54b02" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304960 1074 "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}L{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)}=L{2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)} \\\\ L{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)}=L{-3{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}sL{{x}_{1}(t)}-{x}_{1}(0)=2L{{x}_{1}(t)}+L{{x}_{2}(t)} \\\\ sL{{x}_{2}(t)}-{x}_{2}(0)=-3L{{x}_{1}(t)}+4L{{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+3=2{X}_{1}(s)+{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-2=-3{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304961 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304146 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304961 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304695 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304961 1069 "q-331908" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304961 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304961 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,{X}_{2}(s)=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304961 1072 #uuid "dcaeba59-84e5-5be7-b291-257279aa79dd" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304961 1074 "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}L{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)}=L{2{x}_{1}(t)} \\\\ L{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)}=L{3{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}sL{{x}_{1}(t)}-{x}_{1}(0)=2L{{x}_{1}(t)} \\\\ sL{{x}_{2}(t)}-{x}_{2}(0)=3L{{x}_{1}(t)}+4L{{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-2=2{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+1=3{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}.$\n\nNow, solving the first equation for ${X}_{1}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\ns{X}_{1}(s)-2=2{X}_{1}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{2}{s-2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the second equation and solving for ${X}_{2}(s),$ we have\n\n$$\ns{X}_{2}(s)+1=3(\\frac{2}{s-2})+4{X}_{2}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{-s+8}{(s-2)(s-4)}.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304962 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304476 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304962 1069 "q-331913" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304962 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304962 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=-{x}^{2}-2x+4.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304962 1072 #uuid "0ace32a8-e575-5ba2-b03a-f8e539a9f41f" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304962 1074 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-2x+4\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(4\\alpha +2\\beta )x+(2\\beta +2\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-2x+4.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 4\\alpha +2\\beta =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\beta +2\\gamma =4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =0,\\gamma =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304963 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304174 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304963 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304474 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304963 1069 "q-331917" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304963 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304963 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,{X}_{2}(s)=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304963 1072 #uuid "4870ad0e-2c84-5bc5-850c-476b6d9ea2b7" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304963 1074 "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}L{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)}=L{4{x}_{1}(t)} \\\\ L{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)}=L{{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}sL{{x}_{1}(t)}-{x}_{1}(0)=4L{{x}_{1}(t)} \\\\ sL{{x}_{2}(t)}-{x}_{2}(0)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}+3L{{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+2=4{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-5={X}_{1}(s)+3{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}.$\n\nNow, solving the first equation for ${X}_{1}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\ns{X}_{1}(s)+2=4{X}_{1}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{1}(s)=-\\frac{2}{s-4}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for ${X}_{2}(s)$ gives\n\n$$\ns{X}_{2}(s)-5=(-\\frac{2}{s-4})+3{X}_{2}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{5s-22}{(s-3)(s-4)}.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304305 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304598 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304964 1069 "q-331918" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304964 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304964 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,{X}_{2}(s)=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304964 1072 #uuid "0c4c84fb-7fb4-580a-bff4-417ed1ebd433" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304964 1074 "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}L{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)}=L{-2{x}_{1}(t)} \\\\ L{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)}=L{-3{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}sL{{x}_{1}(t)}-{x}_{1}(0)=-2L{{x}_{1}(t)} \\\\ sL{{x}_{2}(t)}-{x}_{2}(0)=-3L{{x}_{1}(t)}+4L{{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-3=-2{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+4=-3{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}.$\n\nNow, solving the first equation for ${X}_{1}(s),$ we get \n\n$$\ns{X}_{1}(s)-3=-2{X}_{1}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{3}{s+2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the second equation and solving for ${X}_{2}(s)$ gives\n\n$$\ns{X}_{2}(s)+4=-3(\\frac{3}{s+2})+4{X}_{2}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{-4s-17}{(s-4)(s+2)}.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304965 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304443 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304965 1069 "q-331920" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304965 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304965 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=4{x}^{2}-4x+1.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304965 1072 #uuid "1f6ca4b9-883f-5755-8052-e53a6808c119" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304965 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-4x+1\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(4\\alpha -2\\beta )x+(2\\beta -2\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-4x+1\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 4\\alpha -2\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\beta -2\\gamma =1\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\beta =-2,\\gamma =-\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304966 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304471 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304966 1069 "q-331923" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304966 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304966 1071 "A text message has a $20%$ chance of being flagged as spam by a filter. Suppose $10$ messages are randomly selected. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that at least $2$ are flagged as spam?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304966 1072 #uuid "f20ebbbf-5b79-5640-ad87-d933c1a52f9e" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304966 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of messages flagged as spam.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=10$ messages.\n\n- The probability of a message being flagged as spam is $p=20%=0.20.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.20).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.20)^{x}(1-0.20)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.20)^{x}(0.80)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of at least $2$ messages being flagged. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=10)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0})(0.20)^{0}(0.80)^{10-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1})(0.20)^{1}(0.80)^{10-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.80)^{10}+10(0.20)(0.80)^{9}] \\\\  & =1-0.375\\,809… \\\\  & ≈0.624,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304967 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304103 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304967 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304570 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304967 1069 "q-331938" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304967 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304967 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{2}{3},$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}, $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304967 1072 #uuid "c1b2de13-1118-5eab-9243-5697dd077462" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304967 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{2}{3})=-0.729\\,727…≈-0.7297\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=-0.7297$ is not in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ To get the first solution that's within the required domain, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with $-0.7297.$ To do this, we simply add $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-0.7297+2\\pi ≈5.5535\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.7297.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.7297.$\n\n![](images/4a/4a49f40b94a408ea0094eb9f8fd065622e2aad91e56c2eba24071442d0d32e4c.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.7297 \\\\  & ≈3.8713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈3.9,\\,x≈5.6.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304421 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304968 1069 "q-33194" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304968 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304968 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:r=⟨2,1,0⟩+t⟨1,3,2⟩,\\,Π:3x-2y+4z+6=0.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304968 1072 #uuid "8c9e4b1e-64e1-5274-81d9-5dc1e69f7cbb" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304968 1074 "Let's start by writing the equation of the line as\n\n$$\nr=⟨2+t,1+3t,2t⟩.\n$$\n\nLet's also write the equation of the plane in the dot product form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2y+4z+6 & =0 \\\\ 3x-2y+4z & =-6 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-2,4⟩ & =-6 \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-2,4⟩ & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨2+t,1+3t,2t⟩$ into the equation of the plane, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨3,-2,4⟩ & =-6 \\\\ ⟨2+t,1+3t,2t⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-2,4⟩ & =-6 \\\\ 3(2+t)-2(1+3t)+4(2t) & =-6 \\\\ 6+3t-2-6t+8t & =-6 \\\\ 5t & =-10 \\\\ t & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P,$ we substitute $t=-2$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =⟨2+(-2),1+3(-2),2(-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-5,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intersection point is $P(0,-5,-4).$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304969 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304377 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304969 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304395 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304969 1069 "q-331940" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304969 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304969 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},\n$\\,{X}_{2}(s)=$ {{field-2}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304969 1072 #uuid "daf5c29f-5732-5955-b4c0-e9cfb924874e" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304969 1074 "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}L{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)}=L{-3{x}_{1}(t)} \\\\ L{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)}=L{2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}sL{{x}_{1}(t)}-{x}_{1}(0)=-3L{{x}_{1}(t)} \\\\ sL{{x}_{2}(t)}-{x}_{2}(0)=2L{{x}_{1}(t)}+L{{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-1=-3{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-5=2{X}_{1}(s)+{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}.$\n\nNow, solving the first equation for ${X}_{1}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\ns{X}_{1}(s)-1=-3{X}_{1}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{1}{s+3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the second equation and solving for ${X}_{2}(s),$ we have\n\n$$\ns{X}_{2}(s)-5=2(\\frac{1}{s+3})+{X}_{2}(s)\\,⟹\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{5s+17}{(s+3)(s-1)}.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304970 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304223 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304970 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304731 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304970 1069 "q-331941" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304970 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304970 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-0.8,$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}, $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304970 1072 #uuid "cfb1418a-56ae-5d24-8d29-f6801bb2193e" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304970 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-0.8)=-0.927\\,295…≈-0.9273\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=-0.9273$ is not in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ To get the first solution that's within the required domain, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with $-0.9273.$ To do this, we simply add $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-0.9273+2\\pi ≈5.3559\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.9273.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.9273.$\n\n![](images/94/942d3d727557ee280b93b212e856f4a696bcf06438944299fd6596dfb5b147f4.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.9273 \\\\  & ≈4.0689\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are  \n\n$$\nx≈4.1,\\,x≈5.4.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304971 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304303 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304971 1069 "q-331954" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304971 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304971 1071 "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x-{45}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as\n\n$$\nx={15}^{∘}+{72}^{∘}\\cdot n,\\,x=b+{72}^{∘}\\cdot n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $b$ in the range $[{0}^{∘},{72}^{∘}).$\n\n$b=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304971 1072 #uuid "fd36295d-24a0-5457-9e9d-eba4fe370b16" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304971 1074 "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-{45}^{∘} & ={30}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n, & \\, & \\, & 5x-{45}^{∘} & ={150}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & ={30}^{∘}+{45}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & 5x & ={150}^{∘}+{45}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ 5x & ={75}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & 5x & ={195}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n \\\\ x & ={15}^{∘}+{72}^{∘}\\cdot n & \\, & \\, & x & ={39}^{∘}+{72}^{∘}\\cdot n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $b={39}^{∘}.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304972 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304642 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304972 1069 "q-331964" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304972 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304972 1071 "Consider the following trigonometric equation: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\nThe general solution of this equation can be expressed as \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{4\\pi n}{3},\\,x=a\\pi +\\frac{4\\pi n}{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. Find a possible value of $a$ in the interval $[0,\\frac{4}{3}).$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304972 1072 #uuid "a7d53e82-c7ef-5bc3-80c3-62cfaf6b2bb3" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304972 1074 "The general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n, & \\, & \\, & \\frac{3x}{2} & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n & \\, & \\, & \\frac{3x}{2} & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2\\pi n \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{4\\pi n}{3} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+\\frac{4\\pi n}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that $a=\\frac{7}{6}.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304973 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304973 1069 "q-33197" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304973 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304973 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:\\frac{x-2}{-3}=\\frac{y-3}{2}=\\frac{z+4}{-1},\\,Π:3x-2y+z-38=0.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304973 1072 #uuid "00b27684-fae1-56d0-a859-d1fc21cc59df" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304973 1074 "From the equation of the line $l$, we know that it passes through the point $A(2,3,-4)$ and it is parallel to the vector $v=⟨-3,2,-1⟩.$ Therefore, its parametric equations are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨2,3,-4⟩+t⟨-3,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2-3t,3+2t,-4-t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nLet's also write the equation of the plane in the dot product form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2y+z-38 & =0 \\\\ 3x-2y+z & =38 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-2,1⟩ & =38 \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-2,1⟩ & =38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨2-3t,3+2t,-4-t⟩$ into the equation of the plane, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨3,-2,1⟩ & =38 \\\\ ⟨2-3t,3+2t,-4-t⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-2,1⟩ & =38 \\\\ 3(2-3t)-2(3+2t)+(-4-t) & =38 \\\\ 6-9t-6-4t-4-t & =38 \\\\ -14t & =42 \\\\ t & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P,$ we substitute $t=-3$ into the vector equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =⟨2-3(-3),3+2(-3),-4-(-3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨11,-3,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get $P(11,-3,-1).$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304974 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304488 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304974 1069 "q-331974" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304974 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304974 1071 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-9{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304974 1072 #uuid "164f3277-5bbd-5470-9288-18ef536cb19b" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304974 1074 "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-9{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=6{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304975 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304284 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304975 1069 "q-331976" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304975 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304975 1071 "Which of the following systems of linear ODEs are decoupled?\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-12{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304975 1072 #uuid "cfb33e06-625f-5894-a78d-d1f418d2b822" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304975 1074 "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System I is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-12{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System II is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System III is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+6{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304976 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304337 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304976 1069 "q-331989" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304976 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304976 1071 "Find the general solution to the matrix differential equation $\\,{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]x(t).$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304976 1072 #uuid "69caff5b-f7ed-5470-8207-7540a07ed223" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304976 1074 "Given a decoupled system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix of our differential equation is diagonal, the system is decoupled. \n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{10t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304977 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304513 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304977 1069 "q-33201" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304977 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304977 1071 "Find the intersection point of the line $l$ and the plane $Π$ with equations\n\n$$\nl:r=⟨3,0,-4⟩+t⟨2,4,3⟩,\\,Π:4x+3y+4z-60=0.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304977 1072 #uuid "c249fd8c-dcff-5201-89e1-bb6d2a206072" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304977 1074 "Let's start by writing the equation of the line as\n\n$$\nr=⟨3+2t,4t,-4+3t⟩.\n$$\n\nLet's also write the equation of the plane in the dot product form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+3y+4z-60 & =0 \\\\ 4x+3y+4z & =60 \\\\ ⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨4,3,4⟩ & =60 \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨4,3,4⟩ & =60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $r=⟨3+2t,4t,-4+3t⟩$ into the equation of the plane, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\cdot ⟨4,3,4⟩ & =60 \\\\ ⟨3+2t,4t,-4+3t⟩\\cdot ⟨4,3,4⟩ & =60 \\\\ 4(3+2t)+3(4t)+4(-4+3t) & =60 \\\\ 12+8t+12t-16+12t & =60 \\\\ 32t & =64 \\\\ t & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to get the position vector $p$ of the intersection point $P,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =⟨3+2(2),4(2),-4+3(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨7,8,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intersection point is $P(7,8,2).$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304978 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304275 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304978 1069 "q-332017" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304978 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304978 1071 "A data set has mean $\\overset{―}{x}=4$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.25.$ Find the $z$-score of an observation $x=3.4.$\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304978 1072 #uuid "3761b57d-2173-5f10-9590-2a381a947794" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304978 1074 "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nHere, we're told that the mean is $\\overset{―}{x}=4,$ and we can calculate the standard deviation as\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{0.25}=0.5.\n$$\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=3.4$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & =\\frac{3.4-4}{0.5} \\\\  & =-1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304979 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304285 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304979 1069 "q-332018" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304979 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304979 1071 "A data set has mean $\\overset{―}{x}=7$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1.7.$ Find the $z$-score of an observation $x=6.2,$ rounding your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304979 1072 #uuid "a4b40db0-6db4-5a91-bc58-33a9cb3fb0da" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304979 1074 "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nHere, we're told that the mean is $\\overset{―}{x}=7,$ and we can calculate the standard deviation as\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{1.7}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=6.2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & =\\frac{6.2-7}{\\sqrt{1.7}} \\\\  & ≈-0.61,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304980 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304711 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304980 1069 "q-332024" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304980 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304980 1071 "A data set has mean $\\overset{―}{x}=-1.2$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.32.$ Find the $z$-score of an observation $x=-2.1,$ rounding your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304980 1072 #uuid "b9e69acc-e1d3-59d6-aefe-90a346a2a824" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304980 1074 "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nHere, we're told that the mean is $\\overset{―}{x}=-1.2,$ and we can calculate the standard deviation as\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{0.32}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=-2.1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & =\\frac{-2.1-(-1.2)}{\\sqrt{0.32}} \\\\  & ≈-1.59,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304981 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304192 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304981 1069 "q-332033" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304981 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304981 1071 "A data set has a standard deviation $\\sigma =2.5$ and an unknown mean. An observation of the data set has the value $x=11$ and $z$-score $z=0.8.$ Find the mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ of the data set.\n\n$\\overset{―}{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304981 1072 #uuid "1ab12fbc-aef3-51d7-bf81-34394a37c73d" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304981 1074 "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=11,$ $z=0.8,$ and $\\sigma =2.5$ into the formula and solving for $\\overset{―}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 11 & =\\overset{―}{x}+0.8\\cdot 2.5 \\\\ 11 & =\\overset{―}{x}+2 \\\\ \\overset{―}{x} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304982 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304315 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304982 1069 "q-332047" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304982 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304982 1071 "A data set has a mean $\\overset{―}{x}=10$ and an unknown standard deviation. An observation of the data set has the value $x=12.1$ and $z$-score $z=1.05.$ Find the standard deviation of the data set.\n\n$\\sigma =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304982 1072 #uuid "3acea4ed-6e0d-54ee-b704-857befc4faa6" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304982 1074 "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=12.1,$ $\\overset{―}{x}=10,$ and $z=1.05$ into the formula and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 12.1 & =10+1.05\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 2.1 & =1.05\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304983 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304709 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304983 1069 "q-332054" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304983 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304983 1071 "A data set has a mean $\\overset{―}{x}=-2$ and an unknown standard deviation. An observation of the data set has the value $x=-1$ and $z$-score $z=1.25.$ Find the standard deviation of the data set.\n\n$\\sigma =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304983 1072 #uuid "b8399e95-1d96-5965-b7ec-c72c0ae4a5de" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304983 1074 "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1,$ $\\overset{―}{x}=-2,$ and $z=1.25$ into the formula and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ -1 & =-2+1.25\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 1 & =1.25\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304984 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304410 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304984 1069 "q-332057" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304984 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304984 1071 "A data set has a mean $\\overset{―}{x}=6.2$ and an unknown standard deviation. An observation of the data set has the value $x=5.08$ and $z$-score $z=-0.7.$ Find the standard deviation of the data set.\n\n$\\sigma =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304984 1072 #uuid "65e75a1e-5aa4-58f0-8bfb-9a102ccbd14b" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304984 1074 "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5.08,$ $\\overset{―}{x}=6.2,$ and $z=-0.7$ into the formula and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 5.08 & =6.2+(-0.7)\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ -1.12 & =-0.7\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304985 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304411 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304985 1069 "q-332059" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304985 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304985 1071 "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L{f(t)}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L{{e}^{5t}f(t)}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304985 1072 #uuid "a494f0da-488f-5d2c-a912-87d73ea366c9" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304985 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L{{e}^{5t}f(t)},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $5$ units to the right." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304986 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304272 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304986 1069 "q-332063" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304986 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304986 1071 "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L{f(t)}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L{{e}^{8t}f(t)}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304986 1072 #uuid "f3bbef6f-6296-5a54-8079-8df45d564e16" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304986 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L{{e}^{8t}f(t)},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $8$ units to the right." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304987 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304689 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304987 1069 "q-332067" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304987 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304987 1071 "Let $f(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Find ${h}^{′}(\\pi )$ given the function $h$ is defined by $h(x)={\\int }_{x}^{2\\pi }f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ for $0<x<2\\pi .$\n\n${h}^{′}(\\pi )=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304987 1072 #uuid "3cf43180-bbfa-521c-a5f2-0ebd6240c769" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304987 1074 "In this example, the variable limit $x$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we prefer the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\nh(x)={\\int }_{x}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-{\\int }_{2\\pi }^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{2\\pi }^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{2\\pi }^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of ${h}^{′}(\\pi ),$ we substitute $x=\\pi $ in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\pi )\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi \\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304988 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304099 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304988 1069 "q-332071" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304988 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304988 1071 "Let $f(t)=2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-1.$ Find ${h}^{′}(0)$ given the function $h$ is defined by $h(x)={\\int }_{x}^{1}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ for $x<1.$\n\n${h}^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304988 1072 #uuid "33c37cdb-d9b7-5113-b993-0ee12fc9e8de" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304988 1074 "In this example, the variable limit $x$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\nh(x)={\\int }_{x}^{1}(2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t=-{\\int }_{1}^{x}(2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{x}^{1}(2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{1}^{x}(2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{1}^{x}(2{t}^{3}+{t}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-(2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of ${h}^{′}(0),$ we substitute $x=0$ in the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(0) & =-(2\\cdot {0}^{3}+{0}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =-(0+0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304989 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304121 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304989 1069 "q-332116" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304989 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304989 1071 "![](images/9c/9ca5e0c34ea6514a73a1c16147b2c3e71bbf686fb874f92eb18817d3a8c6e0bc.png)\n\nConsider a function $f(t)$ for which the graph of the Laplace transform $L{f(t)}=F(s)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $L{{e}^{6t}f(t)}?$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304989 1072 #uuid "f405a29d-12ba-5a27-b6d9-2ea86d0cc6eb" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304989 1074 "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L{f(t)}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L{{e}^{6t}f(t)},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $6$ units to the right.\n\n![](images/31/3171a91443332410985bf7e42d54719185c884b9183e55e4ba7e574d6c0b2143.png)" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304555 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304990 1069 "q-332133" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304990 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304990 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=5{t}^{2}+9t{e}^{3t}$ for $s>3.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304990 1072 #uuid "8e2260d5-2c33-5f8d-aede-72fd6d295719" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304990 1074 "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{5{t}^{2}+9t{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =L{5{t}^{2}}+L{9t{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =5L{{t}^{2}}+9L{t{e}^{3t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ L{{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L{f(t)}=F(s),$ then $L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nL{t{e}^{3t}}=\\frac{1}{(s-3)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =5L{{t}^{2}}+9L{t{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+9\\cdot \\frac{1}{(s-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{9}{(s-3)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>3.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304991 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304577 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304991 1069 "q-332138" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304991 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304991 1071 "Find the **exact** value of the expression below.\n\n${cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{7\\pi }{8})-{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{7\\pi }{8})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304991 1072 #uuid "7557cacf-886f-55fb-9076-1837e9a0913c" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304991 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{7\\pi }{8})-{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{7\\pi }{8}) & =cos\\,(2\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =cos\\,(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304992 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304588 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304992 1069 "q-332139" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304992 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304992 1071 "Find the **exact** value of the expression below.\n\n${cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})-{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304992 1072 #uuid "24bac5e7-d993-51f0-aa0a-781406c4bd83" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304992 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})-{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}) & =cos\\,(2\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =cos\\,(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304548 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304993 1069 "q-332147" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304993 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304993 1071 "Find the **exact** value of the expression below.\n\n$1-2{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304993 1072 #uuid "64b6ca5b-7886-5c02-8389-aebf02e6026c" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304993 1074 "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304994 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304282 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304994 1069 "q-332148" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304994 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304994 1071 "Find the **exact** value of the expression below.\n\n${cos}^{4}⁡({67.5}^{∘})-2{cos}^{2}⁡({67.5}^{∘}){sin}^{2}⁡({67.5}^{∘})+{sin}^{4}⁡({67.5}^{∘})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304994 1072 #uuid "7667b041-07ed-5199-9efb-833c8da623ff" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304994 1074 "First, notice that our expression is a perfect square that can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{4}⁡({67.5}^{∘})-2{cos}^{2}⁡({67.5}^{∘}){sin}^{2}⁡({67.5}^{∘})+{sin}^{4}⁡({67.5}^{∘}) & ={({cos}^{2}⁡({67.5}^{∘})-{sin}^{2}⁡({67.5}^{∘}))}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={67.5}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡({67.5}^{∘})-{sin}^{2}⁡({67.5}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {67.5}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({135}^{∘}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({cos}^{2}⁡({67.5}^{∘})-{sin}^{2}⁡({67.5}^{∘}))}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304304 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304995 1069 "q-332150" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304995 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304995 1071 "Find the **exact** value of the expression below.\n\n${cos}^{4}\\,(\\frac{7\\pi }{12})-2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}){sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{7\\pi }{12})+{sin}^{4}\\,(\\frac{7\\pi }{12})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304995 1072 #uuid "f3454171-a1b3-502e-bdda-985c2af86469" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304995 1074 "First, notice that our expression is a perfect square that can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{4}\\,(\\frac{7\\pi }{12})-2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}){sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{7\\pi }{12})+{sin}^{4}\\,(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}) & ={({cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{7\\pi }{12})-{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}))}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{7\\pi }{12})-{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}) & =cos\\,(2\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{12}) \\\\  & =cos\\,(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{7\\pi }{12})-{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{7\\pi }{12}))}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304996 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304753 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304996 1069 "q-332151" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304996 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304996 1071 "Find the **exact** value of the expression below.\n\n${cos}^{4}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+{sin}^{4}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304996 1072 #uuid "0d3b4ee6-98d1-5207-a0a1-2482e40fe54d" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304996 1074 "First, notice that our expression is a perfect square that can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{4}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+{sin}^{4}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={({cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =cos\\,(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-{sin}^{2}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))}^{2} & ={0}^{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304997 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304139 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304997 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304255 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304997 1069 "q-332173" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304997 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304997 1071 "Express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{330}^{∘})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{330}^{∘})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304997 1072 #uuid "07e8f887-fa5d-5bbf-ab27-cf58c1880192" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304997 1074 "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v={330}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+{330}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{330}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{330}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304998 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304348 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304998 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304434 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304998 1069 "q-332175" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304998 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304998 1071 "Express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{210}^{∘})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{210}^{∘})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304998 1072 #uuid "5f32cff9-3a46-5a4a-8317-12474c36f089" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304998 1074 "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v={210}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{210}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{210}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{210}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304697 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304999 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304704 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304999 1069 "q-332178" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304999 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304999 1071 "Express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{300}^{∘})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{300}^{∘})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304999 1072 #uuid "bfc5afd1-9723-5b19-a1e3-57f1ad1233ac" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186304999 1074 "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v={300}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{300}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{300}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{300}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305000 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304586 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305000 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304759 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305000 1069 "q-332180" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305000 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305000 1071 "Express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{330}^{∘})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{330}^{∘})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305000 1072 #uuid "da4f3b0b-e716-525b-97e5-9d6526f804b7" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305000 1074 "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v={330}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{330}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{330}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{330}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305001 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304504 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305001 1069 "q-332183" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305001 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305001 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{4t}+5{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}$ for $s>4.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305001 1072 #uuid "75147345-f194-5790-8fe4-ff587f8030d2" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305001 1074 "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{4t}+5{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{4t}}+L{5{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}} \\\\  & =L{{e}^{4t}}+5L{{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}} & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L{f(t)}=F(s),$ then $L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}} & =\\frac{6}{(s+1)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{{e}^{4t}}+5L{{t}^{3}{e}^{-t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4}+5\\cdot \\frac{6}{(s+1)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-4}+\\frac{30}{(s+1)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>4.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305002 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304638 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305002 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304639 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305002 1069 "q-332188" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305002 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305002 1071 "Express $sin\\,(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{4})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$sin\\,(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305002 1072 #uuid "87336710-8c90-5846-a70d-1640898f0eff" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305002 1074 "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin\\,(x+\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{7\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305003 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304523 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305003 1069 "q-332190" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305003 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305003 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=5{e}^{-t}+3{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}$ for $s>2.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305003 1072 #uuid "f73d8b5a-dd22-5077-ae69-38f16e9a5efa" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305003 1074 "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{5{e}^{-t}+3{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =L{5{e}^{-t}}+L{3{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =5L{{e}^{-t}}+3L{{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}} & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L{f(t)}=F(s),$ then $L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}} & =\\frac{6}{(s-2)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =5L{{e}^{-t}}+3L{{t}^{3}{e}^{2t}} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+1}+3\\cdot \\frac{6}{(s-2)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{s+1}+\\frac{18}{(s-2)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>2.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305004 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304157 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305004 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304306 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305004 1069 "q-332191" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305004 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305004 1071 "Express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{7\\pi }{6})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{7\\pi }{6})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305004 1072 #uuid "78623ec6-7143-54b5-8ec3-a1bf17721f5e" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305004 1074 "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305005 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304105 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305005 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304283 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305005 1069 "q-332195" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305005 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305005 1071 "Express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305005 1072 #uuid "f6d9223e-755f-520e-aa29-e860e8e723c3" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305005 1074 "Recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for sine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-\\frac{5\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305006 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304671 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305006 1069 "q-332199" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305006 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305006 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=6{e}^{2t}+2{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}$ for $s>2.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305006 1072 #uuid "d34dedd9-0917-526a-8fa5-cdd60c269ceb" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305006 1074 "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{6{e}^{2t}+2{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}} \\\\  & =L{6{e}^{2t}}+L{2{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}} \\\\  & =6L{{e}^{2t}}+2L{{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{n}} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}} & =\\frac{6}{{s}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L{f(t)}=F(s),$ then $L{{e}^{at}f(t)}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}} & =\\frac{6}{(s+4)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =6L{{e}^{2t}}+2L{{t}^{3}{e}^{-4t}} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-2}+2\\cdot \\frac{6}{(s+4)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{s-2}+\\frac{12}{(s+4)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>2.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305007 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305007 1069 "q-332211" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305007 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305007 1071 "A data set has a mean $\\overset{―}{x}=-0.3$ and a standard deviation $\\sigma =0.5.$ An observation of the data set has a $z$-score $z=1.4.$ Find the value $x$ of this observation.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305007 1072 #uuid "5a014c71-9d18-55ba-a9d1-8dde396d4afc" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305007 1074 "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\overset{―}{x}=-0.3,$ $z=1.4,$ and $\\sigma =0.5$ into the formula and solving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ x & =-0.3+1.4\\cdot 0.5 \\\\ x & =-0.3+0.7 \\\\ x & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305008 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304207 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305008 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304212 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305008 1069 "q-332213" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305008 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305008 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0.85,$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$, $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305008 1072 #uuid "a871a980-4f80-5fb7-b6b2-606a51c29403" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305008 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0.85)=58.211\\,669…≈{58.2117}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSince ${58.2117}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={58.2117}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={58.2117}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={58.2117}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/ec/ec4366c4e46cc9c1df5fb31aa2ca04f337fd3c6d0bce034405ca2b48874aa32a.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{58.2117}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈{121.7883}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈{58.2}^{∘},\\,x≈{121.8}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305009 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304221 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305009 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304750 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305009 1069 "q-332214" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305009 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305009 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-0.4,$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$, $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305009 1072 #uuid "9bc16736-0643-5460-bc19-737cf1bfebb4" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305009 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-0.4)=-23.578\\,178{…}^{∘}≈-{23.578}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=-{23.578}^{∘}$ is not in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ To get the first solution that's within the required domain, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with $-{23.578}^{∘}.$ To do this, we simply add ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-{23.578}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={336.422}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={23.578}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={23.578}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/c5/c587e119531c3363816f72e9f2e1acdcc1aba87f278caa658342cbff144b0dad.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{23.578}^{∘} \\\\  & ={203.578}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈{204}^{∘},\\,x≈{336}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304077 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304126 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305010 1069 "q-332216" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305010 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305010 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-0.7,$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$, $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305010 1072 #uuid "01dbb5eb-fd22-5b08-b7a2-d6de5f372dc7" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305010 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-0.7)=-44.427\\,004{…}^{∘}≈-{44.4270}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=-{44.4270}^{∘}$ is not in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ To get the first solution that's within the required domain, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with $-{44.4270}^{∘}.$ To do this, we simply add ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-{44.4270}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={315.5730}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={44.4270}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={44.4270}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/f3/f3d2d41e1a58dd562ed47ae7e5fcdc79382c426700f520a1153111bfcf2d734b.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{44.4270}^{∘} \\\\  & ={224.4270}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈{224.4}^{∘},\\,x≈{315.6}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305011 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304656 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305011 1069 "q-332233" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305011 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305011 1071 "A data set contains the observations ${x}_{1}=-1.2,{x}_{2}=2.8$ with $z$-scores ${z}_{1}=-0.5$ and ${z}_{2}=2,$ respectively. Find the mean $\\overset{¯}{x}$ of the data set.\n\n$\\overset{¯}{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305011 1072 #uuid "462eb062-e9a4-56a4-b267-9be7a733e58a" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305011 1074 "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nFrom the first observation, we have\n\n$$\n-1.2=\\overset{¯}{x}+(-0.5)\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nFrom the second observation, we have\n\n$$\n2.8=\\overset{¯}{x}+2\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.8-(-1.2) & =(2-(-0.5))\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 4 & =2.5\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this value back into our first equation and solving for $\\overset{¯}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1.2 & =\\overset{¯}{x}+(-0.5)\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ -1.2 & =\\overset{¯}{x}+(-0.5)\\cdot 1.6 \\\\ -1.2 & =\\overset{¯}{x}-0.8 \\\\ \\overset{¯}{x} & =-0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305012 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304341 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305012 1069 "q-332238" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305012 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305012 1071 "A data set contains the observations ${x}_{1}=-6.24,{x}_{2}=-1.64$ with $z$-scores ${z}_{1}=-0.8$ and ${z}_{2}=1.2,$ respectively. Find the mean $\\overset{¯}{x}$ of the data set.\n\n$\\overset{¯}{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305012 1072 #uuid "a0d4eefe-c238-5c62-90b4-32faf1499a6c" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305012 1074 "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nFrom the first observation, we have\n\n$$\n-6.24=\\overset{¯}{x}+(-0.8)\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nFrom the second observation, we have\n\n$$\n-1.64=\\overset{¯}{x}+1.2\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1.64-(-6.24) & =(1.2-(-0.8))\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 4.6 & =2\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =2.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this value back into our first equation and solving for $\\overset{¯}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6.24 & =\\overset{¯}{x}+(-0.8)\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ -6.24 & =\\overset{¯}{x}+(-0.8)\\cdot 2.3 \\\\ -6.24 & =\\overset{¯}{x}-1.84 \\\\ \\overset{¯}{x} & =-4.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305013 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304611 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305013 1069 "q-332239" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305013 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305013 1071 "A data set contains the observations ${x}_{1}=3.08,{x}_{2}=8.68$ with $z$-scores ${z}_{1}=-1.2$ and ${z}_{2}=2.3,$ respectively. Find the mean $\\overset{¯}{x}$ of the data set.\n\n$\\overset{¯}{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305013 1072 #uuid "cbfb0339-7153-551d-aafb-c429f4a2a68d" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305013 1074 "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nFrom the first observation, we have\n\n$$\n3.08=\\overset{¯}{x}+(-1.2)\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nFrom the second observation, we have\n\n$$\n8.68=\\overset{¯}{x}+2.3\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8.68-3.08 & =(2.3-(-1.2))\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 5.6 & =3.5\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this value back into our first equation and solving for $\\overset{¯}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.08 & =\\overset{¯}{x}+(-1.2)\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 3.08 & =\\overset{¯}{x}+(-1.2)\\cdot 1.6 \\\\ 3.08 & =\\overset{¯}{x}-1.92 \\\\ \\overset{¯}{x} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305014 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304383 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305014 1069 "q-332248" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305014 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305014 1071 "A data set contains the observations ${x}_{1}=0.5,{x}_{2}=4.5$ with $z$-scores ${z}_{1}=-3$ and ${z}_{2}=5,$ respectively. Find the standard deviation $\\sigma $ of the data set.\n\n$\\sigma =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305014 1072 #uuid "d8b8af68-19d0-567b-8589-37d341605a02" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305014 1074 "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nFrom the first observation, we have\n\n$$\n0.5=\\overset{¯}{x}+(-3)\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nFrom the second observation, we have\n\n$$\n4.5=\\overset{¯}{x}+5\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4.5-0.5 & =(5-(-3))\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 4 & =8\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305015 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304169 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305015 1069 "q-332254" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305015 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305015 1071 "The mean score on a national mathematics contest is $\\overset{¯}{x}=80$ points with a standard deviation of $5$ points. What is the $z\\text{-score}$ that corresponds to a student's score of $65$ points?\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305015 1072 #uuid "8c902084-01f8-5c25-bb7a-c7a34048e719" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305015 1074 "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=65$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{¯}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & =\\frac{65-80}{5} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305016 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304698 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305016 1069 "q-332257" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305016 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305016 1071 "The mean heart rate of a group of patients during light exercise is $\\overset{¯}{x}=70$ beats per minute. A particular patient in this group has a heart rate of $74$ beats per minute, and his $z$-score is $z=2.5.$ Find the standard deviation of the patients' heart rates in the group.\n\nThe standard deviation of the heart rates is\n{{field-1}}\nbeats per minute." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305016 1072 #uuid "8c51640f-79ce-5917-bf0e-3857534e2a0b" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305016 1074 "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\overset{―}{x}=70,$ $z=2.5,$ and $x=74$ into the formula and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 74 & =70+2.5\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 4 & =2.5\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{4}{2.5} \\\\ \\sigma  & =1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard deviation of the patients' heart rates in the group is $1.6$ beats per minute." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305017 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304527 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305017 1069 "q-332274" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305017 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305017 1071 "In a certain video game, the mean score of all players is $\\overset{¯}{x}=10$ points with a standard deviation of $1.5$ points. A particular player's $z$-score is $z=2.$ Find this player's score $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305017 1072 #uuid "5a8a2e99-9f41-5e5e-bc48-de3e2b627c34" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305017 1074 "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\overset{―}{x}=10,$ $z=2,$ and $\\sigma =1.5$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ x & =10+2\\cdot 1.5 \\\\ x & =10+3 \\\\ x & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305018 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304705 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305018 1069 "q-332289" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305018 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305018 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+5y=15.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305018 1072 #uuid "eb6fc58a-1ee1-5b56-ab52-7c9e03233022" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305018 1074 "The right-hand side of our inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+5\\alpha =15\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305019 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304133 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305019 1069 "q-332292" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305019 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305019 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=-10.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305019 1072 #uuid "0c3d0dd3-8688-50cf-b970-bea1aed01a3b" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305019 1074 "The right-hand side of our inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-2\\alpha =-10\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305020 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304400 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305020 1069 "q-332295" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305020 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305020 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=-20.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305020 1072 #uuid "24a19ea3-a458-5e4a-9e3f-e4bd6247fa61" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305020 1074 "The right-hand side of our inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-5\\alpha =-20\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305021 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304163 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305021 1069 "q-332302" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305021 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305021 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+8y=8x+9.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305021 1072 #uuid "9545e776-71a5-52fa-8fbb-80c35c8bda51" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305021 1074 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +8(\\alpha x+\\beta )=8x+9\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n8\\alpha x+(\\alpha +8\\beta )=8x+9.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}8\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +8\\beta =9\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x+1.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305022 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304628 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305022 1069 "q-332305" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305022 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305022 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y=-4x-7.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305022 1072 #uuid "c4c59710-9816-544a-ae48-e9731e281920" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305022 1074 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha -4(\\alpha x+\\beta )=-4x-7\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-4\\alpha x+(\\alpha -4\\beta )=-4x-7.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -4\\beta =-7\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\,\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x+2.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305023 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304425 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305023 1069 "q-332307" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305023 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305023 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=-6x-7.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305023 1072 #uuid "8b882e96-167b-50d7-8f21-ffd3effbc9e6" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305023 1074 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +6(\\alpha x+\\beta )=-6x-7\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n6\\alpha x+(\\alpha +6\\beta )=-6x-7.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6\\alpha =-6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +6\\beta =-7\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\,\\beta =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-x-1.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305024 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304183 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305024 1069 "q-332311" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305024 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305024 1071 "A customer service representative resolves a caller’s issue with a $45%$ chance during each minute of a call. The call continues until the issue is resolved. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that it will take exactly $5$ minutes to resolve the issue is{{field-1}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305024 1072 #uuid "a0f07871-e078-5fd1-adbb-80cf32289f4b" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305024 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of minutes until the representative resolves the issue. Since the chance of resolving the issue in any given minute is $45%,$ the number of minutes until the issue is resolved can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=45%=\\frac{45}{100}=0.45.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.45).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.45)^{x-1}(0.45) \\\\  & =(0.55)^{x-1}(0.45)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the representative will resolve the issue for the first time in the $5$th minute is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =(0.55)^{5-1}(0.45) \\\\  & =(0.55)^{4}(0.45) \\\\  & ≈0.041,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305025 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304464 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305025 1069 "q-332312" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305025 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305025 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-{x}^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305025 1072 #uuid "33e0c74a-9252-5ce6-b3a3-6d3dce732cbe" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305025 1074 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha +\\beta )x+(\\beta +\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta +\\gamma =3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =2,\\gamma =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+2x+1.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305026 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304645 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305026 1069 "q-332319" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305026 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305026 1071 "A bag contains $10$ balls numbered $1$ through $10.$ One ball is randomly selected, its number is recorded, and then it is placed back in the bag before the next selection. The process is repeated until the ball numbered $10$ is selected. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that it will take exactly $5$ selections is{{field-1}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305026 1072 #uuid "720d9b9e-459a-5820-84a2-68055ba82c56" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305026 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of selections until the ball numbered $10$ came on. The probability of getting a ball numbered $10$ on any single selection is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{1}{10}.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of selections until the ball numbered $10$ came up can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=\\frac{1}{10}.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{1}{10}).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={(1-\\frac{1}{10})}^{x-1}(\\frac{1}{10}) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{9}{10})}^{x-1}(\\frac{1}{10})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of getting a ball numbered $10$ on the $5$th selection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{9}{10})}^{5-1}(\\frac{1}{10}) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{9}{10})}^{4}(\\frac{1}{10}) \\\\  & ≈0.066,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305027 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304387 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305027 1069 "q-332323" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305027 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305027 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-{x}^{2}-2x-2.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305027 1072 #uuid "2de29351-d047-5e75-944a-ef133c45a0a1" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305027 1074 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-2x-2\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha +\\beta )x+(\\beta +\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-2x-2.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta +\\gamma =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =0,\\gamma =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}-2.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305028 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304717 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305028 1069 "q-332325" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305028 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305028 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y={x}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305028 1072 #uuid "c60668bb-7444-51a7-9d21-131099a908b8" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305028 1074 "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )={x}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(4\\alpha +2\\beta )x+(2\\beta +2\\gamma )={x}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 4\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\beta +2\\gamma =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =-1,\\gamma =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305029 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304752 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305029 1069 "q-332334" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305029 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305029 1071 "A smartphone receives a software update that has a $60%$ chance of installing successfully on any given attempt. The phone keeps trying until the update installs successfully. Rounded to $2$ decimal places, the probability that the update installs successfully within the first $2$ attempts is{{field-1}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305029 1072 #uuid "a868a700-0984-579d-a722-3a92fa3c32e0" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305029 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of attempts until the update installs successfully. Since the chance that the update installs successfully on a single attempt is $60%,$ the number of attempts required to install the update can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=60%=\\frac{60}{100}=0.6.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.6).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.6)^{x-1}(0.6) \\\\  & =(0.4)^{x-1}(0.6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the update installs successfully in one of the first $2$ attempts is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X\\in {1,2}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & =(0.4)^{1-1}(0.6)+(0.4)^{2-1}(0.6) \\\\  & =[1+(0.4)](0.6) \\\\  & =0.84.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305030 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304175 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305030 1069 "q-332342" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305030 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305030 1071 "A company monitors incoming emails for spam. On average, $18$ out of every $150$ emails are spam messages. The emails are checked one by one until a spam email is found. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that a spam email is found before checking the $4$th email is{{field-1}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305030 1072 #uuid "9e7750bc-19d8-582c-842c-1d3d7c48aef5" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305030 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of emails checked until a spam email is found. On average, the chance that any single email is spam is \n\n$$\np=\\frac{18}{150}=0.12.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of emails that are checked before finding a spam email can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=0.12.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.12).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.12)^{x-1}(0.12) \\\\  & =(0.88)^{x-1}(0.12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of finding a spam email before checking $4$ emails is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.88)^{1-1}(0.12)+(0.88)^{2-1}(0.12)+(0.88)^{3-1}(0.12) \\\\  & =(0.12)+(0.88)(0.12)+(0.88)^{2}(0.12) \\\\  & =[1+0.88+(0.88)^{2}](0.12) \\\\  & ≈0.319,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305031 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304561 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305031 1069 "q-332354" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305031 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305031 1071 "A board game uses a special deck of $10$ cards: $4$ cards show a star and $6$ cards show a circle. A player randomly draws a card, records the symbol, and then puts the card back into the deck before drawing again. The player keeps drawing until they get a star card. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability of getting a star card after the third draw is{{field-1}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305031 1072 #uuid "ac860a6a-b0e9-53ea-a402-4f5d52f1de41" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305031 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of cards drawn until a star card is obtained. The chance of selecting a star card from the deck is \n\n$$\np=\\frac{4}{10}=\\frac{2}{5}=0.4.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of selections before obtaining a star card can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{2}{5}).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={(1-\\frac{2}{5})}^{x-1}(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x-1}(\\frac{2}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>3),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>3)=1-P(X\\le 3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{1-1}(\\frac{2}{5})+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2-1}(\\frac{2}{5})+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{3-1}(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{5})+(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{2}{5})+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =[1+(\\frac{3}{5})+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}](\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =[1+\\frac{3}{5}+\\frac{9}{25}](\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =0.784.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of getting a star card after the third draw is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3) & =1-P(X\\le 3) \\\\  & =1-0.784 \\\\  & =0.216.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305032 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304287 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305032 1069 "q-332355" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305032 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305032 1071 "In a medical clinic, a certain rapid test detects a particular infection. On average, $9$ out of every $300$ patients who take the test receive a positive result. Each patient’s test result is independent of the others. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that the nurse will test more than $6$ patients before seeing the first positive result is{{field-1}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305032 1072 #uuid "84805c6a-4e89-58b3-8c55-bd37dc99d79b" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305032 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of patients tested until the first positive result is obtained. Since the chance that any single patient tests positive is $3%,$ the number of patients tested until the first positive result can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=\\frac{9}{300}=0.03.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.03).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.03)^{x-1}(0.03) \\\\  & =(0.97)^{x-1}(0.03)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X>6),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X>6)=1-P(X\\le 6)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4,5,6}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)+f(5)+f(6) \\\\  & =(0.97)^{1-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{2-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{3-1}(0.03) \\\\  & \\,+(0.97)^{4-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{5-1}(0.03)+(0.97)^{6-1}(0.03) \\\\  & =[1+(0.97)+(0.97)^{2}+(0.97)^{3}+(0.97)^{4}+(0.97)^{5}](0.03) \\\\  & ≈0.167,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability that the nurse will test more than $6$ patients before seeing the first positive result is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6) & =1-P(X\\le 6) \\\\  & ≈1-0.167 \\\\  & ≈0.833,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305033 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304314 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305033 1069 "q-332356" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305033 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305033 1071 "A coffee shop tracks how often customers order a latte. On average, $30%$ of customers order a latte. Each customer’s order is independent of the others. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that the first customer who orders a latte is at least the $4$th customer is{{field-1}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305033 1072 #uuid "15bd2ebc-aae7-50fc-abaa-6160869e5ec9" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305033 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of customers served until a latte is ordered. On average, the chance that any single customer orders a latte is \n\n$$\np=30%=\\frac{30}{100}=0.3.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of customers before the first latte order can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=0.3.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.3).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={(1-0.3)}^{x-1}(0.3) \\\\  & ={(0.7)}^{x-1}(0.3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X\\ge 4),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 4)=1-P(X\\le 3)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & =(0.7)^{1-1}(0.3)+(0.7)^{2-1}(0.3)+(0.7)^{3-1}(0.3) \\\\  & =(0.3)+(0.7)(0.3)+(0.7)^{2}(0.3) \\\\  & =[1+0.7+(0.7)^{2}](0.3) \\\\  & =0.657.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the first customer who orders a latte is at least the $4$th customer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 4) & =1-P(X\\le 3) \\\\  & =1-0.657 \\\\  & =0.343.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305034 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304078 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305034 1069 "q-332361" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305034 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305034 1071 "A parcel delivery company records whether each delivered package requires a signature upon arrival. On average, $9$ out of $50$ packages require a signature. Each package's status is independent of the others. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability that the first package requiring a signature is the fifth package delivered or later is{{field-1}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305034 1072 #uuid "1f83c3f1-6bc9-5d24-930b-3c30645c3748" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305034 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of packages delivered until a package that requires a signature is delivered. On average, the chance that any single package requires a signature is \n\n$$\np=\\frac{9}{50}=0.18.\n$$\n\nSo, the number of packages until the first package that requires a signature is delivered can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success $p=0.18.$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.18).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={(1-0.18)}^{x-1}(0.18) \\\\  & ={(0.82)}^{x-1}(0.18)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute $P(X\\ge 5),$ we will use the complement:\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 5)=1-P(X<5)\n$$\n\nComputing the probability of the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(0.82)^{1-1}(0.18)+(0.82)^{2-1}(0.18)+(0.82)^{3-1}(0.18)+(0.82)^{4-1}(0.18) \\\\  & =(0.18)+(0.82)(0.18)+(0.82)^{2}(0.18)+(0.82)^{3}(0.18) \\\\  & =[1+0.82+(0.82)^{2}+(0.82)^{3}](0.18) \\\\  & ≈0.548,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the probability that at least $4$ packages are delivered before the first package that requires a signature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =1-P(X<5) \\\\  & ≈1-0.548 \\\\  & ≈0.452,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305035 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304542 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305035 1069 "q-332367" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305035 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305035 1071 "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{2}{7}),$ compute $P(X=4).$  Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places.\n\n$P(X=4)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305035 1072 #uuid "4fab6883-0393-5f3c-90fe-c90066e575a7" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305035 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{2}{7}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{5}{7})}^{x-1}(\\frac{2}{7}), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=4) & =f(4) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5}{7})}^{3}(\\frac{2}{7}) \\\\  & ≈0.1041.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305036 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304567 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305036 1069 "q-332376" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305036 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305036 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=9x-3.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305036 1072 #uuid "7d4ca729-0adc-56ff-b19a-59462b6f493c" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305036 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha -3(\\alpha x+\\beta )=9x-3\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-3\\alpha x+(\\alpha -3\\beta )=9x-3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha =9\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -3\\beta =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-3,\\,\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3x.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}-3x.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305037 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304191 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305037 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304392 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305037 1069 "q-332377" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305037 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305037 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305037 1072 #uuid "8b86135d-d2a6-5d52-b069-fcc60a9a34a2" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305037 1074 "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2})=-{30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $-{30}^{∘}$ lies inside the domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-{30}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/1b/1b1a3d9a84ba0d6da8d5c2c21a91a6027b1b57e520be7c886ecf5da1a9de1ee0.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={210}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we simply subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =X-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={210}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{150}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are:\n\n$$\nx=-{150}^{∘},\\,x=-{30}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304079 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305038 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304236 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305038 1069 "q-332380" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305038 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305038 1071 "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{3}{5},$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$  are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305038 1072 #uuid 