m above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305108 1072 #uuid "99f51d11-81a2-56e9-b9be-d1b95ecef89b" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305108 1074 "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing away from the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust also approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}>0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}>0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305109 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304581 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305109 1069 "q-332714" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305109 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305109 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+9y=6x-5.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305109 1072 #uuid "e306d610-f26f-5231-9b1a-914db8b88488" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305109 1074 "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+9{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-9{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-9\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -9\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int -9\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =-9x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{-9x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{-9x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{-9x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-9x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305110 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304571 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305110 1069 "q-332716" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305110 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305110 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{7}\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305110 1072 #uuid "57fdddd2-2188-57ae-bf71-330644103608" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305110 1074 "We have a decoupled system. Therefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{7}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{2}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{7}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =9\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{2}^{-t}+(-5)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{7}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{2}^{-t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\sqrt{7}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305111 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304149 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305111 1069 "q-332722" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305111 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305111 1071 "![](images/a6/a6a2a93d3fdf38eb57b5183a8d8c5d87f8524f976b5c8827e4717c0aeaf69200.png)\n\nConsider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305111 1072 #uuid "56352c77-9606-52f0-b0c6-fd071234475d" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305111 1074 "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}<0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-6x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304448 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305112 1069 "q-332727" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305112 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305112 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=10.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305112 1072 #uuid "c4a23120-4c6b-58ce-9c4f-da56dae2e189" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305112 1074 "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=5{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=5\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 5\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int 5\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =5x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{5x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{5x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305113 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304129 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305113 1069 "q-332728" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305113 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305113 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=8{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305113 1072 #uuid "332fe216-05e6-5450-9057-0e365b259ef5" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305113 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{5x},$ and ${e}^{5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{5x}+5x{e}^{5x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x})-5\\alpha x{e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ (1+5x-5x)\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{5x}.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305114 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304124 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305114 1069 "q-332729" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305114 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305114 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=-6.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305114 1072 #uuid "73a4f0da-37cc-5d2d-881f-5ead159fcea4" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305114 1074 "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-4{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-4\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int -4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =-4x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{-4x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305115 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304700 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305115 1069 "q-332730" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305115 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305115 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=8{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305115 1072 #uuid "9f0a9b7d-a8ca-55a3-9a38-83e9ea72a8f0" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305115 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{3x},$ and ${e}^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x})-