1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305118 1071 "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{7}{5}\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nA complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}(t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]){e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as \n$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$\nwhere ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305118 1072 #uuid "24c639b5-3938-594c-bf24-83bf8b532823" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305118 1074 "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is constant. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also constant, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined." 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305119 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304469 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305119 1069 "q-332754" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305119 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305119 1071 "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305119 1072 #uuid "d0a48523-0d38-59da-ad30-a645b8c2cc65" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305119 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(4\\alpha +\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha +\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -\\alpha +4\\beta =-5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, $\\beta =3-4\\alpha .$ Substituting into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n-\\alpha +4(3-4\\alpha )=-5\\,⇒\\,\\alpha =1.\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\beta =3-4\\alpha =3-4(1)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305120 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304127 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305120 1069 "q-332756" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305120 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305120 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=-6x+1,\\,y(0)=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=-6x+1\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305120 1072 #uuid "81970480-b4e2-555c-a388-102c3073cc1a" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305120 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +3(\\alpha x+\\beta )=-6x+1\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\alpha x+(\\alpha +3\\beta )=-6x+1\n$$\n\nEquating the 