 assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=5\\alpha {e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\alpha {e}^{5x}-\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =20{e}^{5x} \\\\ 4\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =20{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+5{e}^{5x}.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305129 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304664 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305129 1069 "q-332805" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305129 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305129 1071 "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+2t+3 \\\\ -{t}^{2}+2t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system can be written as $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t),$ where ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution. Determine the form of the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t).$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305129 1072 #uuid "664eb982-2bc6-5ef7-b05c-2c605627127a" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305129 1074 "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of t