n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nWe're told that the first step is calculated using the Runge-Kutta Method, resulting in \n\n$$\ny(1)≈\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\n\nFirst, we compute the value of ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x-y-1 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{8}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{7}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nFrom the first row of the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=-2.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8})-(-2))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{7}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{13}{16}=\\frac{2}{16}+\\frac{13}{16}=\\frac{15}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{7}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ | $\\frac{15}{16}$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{p}-1 \\\\  & =2-\\frac{15}{16}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{7}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ | $\\frac{15}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{1}{16}+8\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{8})-(-2))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (\\frac{21}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-\\frac{7}{8}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $-\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{13}{16}$ | $\\frac{15}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{7}{64}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{7}{64} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y