etermined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x}-3\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =7{e}^{-2x} \\\\ -5\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =7{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-\\frac{7}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{7}{5}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}-\\frac{7}{5}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\frac{7}{5} & =0 \\\\ A & =\\frac{7}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{7}{5}{e}^{3x}-\\frac{7}{5}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304115 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305153 1069 "q-332908" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305153 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305153 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=2{e}^{4x},\\,y(0)=10\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-y=2{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305153 1072 #uuid "516804e6-9850-5a07-aeb9-4a07986f8eaa" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305153 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $2{e}^{4x},$ and ${e}^{4x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=4\\alpha {e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha {e}^{4x}-\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =2{e}^{4x} \\\\ 3\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =2{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=10$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+\\frac{2}{3} & =10 \\\\ A & =\\frac{28}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{28}{3}{e}^{x}+\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{4x}.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305154 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304094 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305154 1069 "q-332909" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305154 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305154 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=-5{e}^{-3x},\\,y(0)=8\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=-5{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305154 1072 #uuid "1e7dd865-a056-5710-ab6d-c5eb9bf72fc6" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305154 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $-5{e}^{-3x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x}+2\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =-5{e}^{-3x} \\\\ -\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =-5{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+5{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=8$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+5 & =8 \\\\ A & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=3{e}^{-2x}+5{e}^{-3x}.\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304194 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304415 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305155 1069 "q-332914" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305155 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305155 1071 "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t)+{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}-14 \\\\ -27\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n| $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\,\\,$ |  | $2$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305155 1072 #uuid "a4beb5a1-2716-50fa-8921-aa7ead951ee6" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305155 1074 "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}-14 \\\\ -27\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also exponential, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=-4{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}-14 \\\\ -27\\end{aligned}] \\\\ -4[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-14 \\\\ -27\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-4a \\\\ -4b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3a \\\\ 5b\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-14 \\\\ -27\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-4a \\\\ -4b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3a-14 \\\\ 5b-27\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3a-14=-4a \\\\ 5b-27=-4b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n3a-14=-4a\\,⇒\\,a=2.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n5b-27=-4b\\,⇒\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304456 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305156 1069 "q-332917" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305156 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305156 1071 "![](images/e8/e80a2dc709410228439b5acffe70a8df5e60a2e593a8dc142d373dc63d95956b.png)\n\nA sketch of the phase space with nullclines and the corresponding direction field of tangents to the solutions of a system of differential equations is shown above (the tangents are not drawn to scale). Which of the following could represent the trajectory starting at the point marked on the graph?" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305156 1072 #uuid "35337a3f-ac92-585a-ac00-7470310565dd" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305156 1074 "Let's sketch the trajectory.\n\n- We start at the given point and go along with the flow (slightly downward and to the right) until the trajectory intersects the $y$-nullcline, where the tangents are horizontal.\n\n![](images/65/659a566b441d61748a82d4c8b67632432656fad3f8af65cd3a6446062c957817.png)\n\n- Next, the flow continues going to the right but then turns upward.\n\n![](images/2e/2e844f0034ee039a7a97c836099caa983eca9c861d66337772203db991d0f07d.png)\n\n- After that, the trajectory keeps going  to the right and slightly upward to infinity.\n\n![](images/b2/b21e1215502adee98e275e59d1a391665f3cc8b75a69b8c5497739d2b919cc9f.png)" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305157 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304271 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305157 1069 "q-332919" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305157 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305157 1071 "![](images/f3/f346ab4bb4f2c84c7cdc3812dd4028af9e175b0aab06fb1a76ea43e234278a42.png)\n\nA sketch of the phase space with nullclines and the corresponding direction field of tangents to the solutions of a system of differential equations is shown above (the tangents are not drawn to scale). Which of the following could represent the trajectory starting at the point marked on the graph?" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305157 1072 #uuid "b72f4852-29a0-51d4-a21f-da69b6a4005c" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true] [17592186305157 1074 "Let's sketch the trajectory.\n\n- We start at the given point and go along with the flow (upward and slightly to the right) until the trajectory intersects the $x$-nullcline, where the tangents are vertical.\n\n![](images/0b/0b10b27b37589dc59c39f74cd800b06aab1e5d8f9087ea75465c701099dc51d4.png)\n\n- Next, the flow continues going upward but then turns to the left. At some point, our trajectory intersects the $y$-nullcline where the  tangents are horizontal.\n\n![](images/0e/0ef35edaa4bc4306be91e7730be651951987e4b49706a59f3a1245281f58f0fc.png)\n\n- After that, the trajectory proceeds almost horizontally to the left, going to infinity.\n\n![](images/48/48d82dd8338c251969e29c2bfb238c6ab0ded096434abacb3ebc47954635cd9e.png)" 13194139533414 17592186045585 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-003894d7-c1f0-5659-bf28-e65dae8b206d" 1